# Chapter 20 Ideal gases — part a: 20.1 to 20.4.
NA_, R_, k_ = 6.02e23, 8.31, 1.38e-23

# ═════════════════════════ 20.1 Particles of a gas (16/18/10) ═════════════════════════
lesson('20.1')
M('E', 1, 'Which is an assumption of the kinetic model of an ideal gas?', 'The molecules have negligible volume compared with the volume of the container', ['The molecules attract each other strongly', 'The molecules are at rest', 'Collisions between molecules lose kinetic energy'], 'The ideal-gas model assumes point-like molecules, no forces except in collisions, elastic collisions and random motion.')
M('E', 1, 'Smoke particles in air, viewed through a microscope, move in a jerky, random way. What is this called?', 'Brownian motion', ['Diffusion', 'Convection', 'Evaporation'], 'The visible smoke particles are bombarded unevenly by unseen, fast-moving air molecules: evidence for molecular motion.')
M('E', 1, 'What causes the random zig-zag motion of a smoke particle in Brownian motion?', 'Uneven bombardment by much smaller, fast-moving air molecules', ['Convection currents in the air', 'The smoke particle\'s own vibration', 'Electric charges on the smoke'], 'At any instant more molecules hit one side than the other, so the particle is pushed in a random direction.')
N('E', 2, 'How many molecules are there in 0.50 mol of carbon dioxide?' + take('NA'), 0.50 * NA_, None, 'N = nN_A = 0.50 × 6.02 × 10²³ = 3.01 × 10²³', ['C1: N = nN_A'], aq='N')
N('E', 2, 'A cylinder contains 9.0 × 10²⁴ molecules of nitrogen. How many moles is this?' + take('NA'), 9.0e24 / NA_, 'mol', f'n = N/N_A = 9.0 × 10²⁴ ÷ 6.02 × 10²³ = {sf(9e24 / NA_)} mol', ['C1: n = N/N_A'], aq='n')
M('E', 1, 'Why is it reasonable to ignore the forces between molecules in a gas at low pressure?', 'The molecules are far apart on average, so the forces are negligible except during collisions', ['Gas molecules have no mass', 'Gas molecules are charged', 'The molecules are at rest'], 'Intermolecular forces act only over short ranges; in a dilute gas the molecules spend most of their time far apart.')
M('E', 1, 'In the kinetic model, how do gas molecules move between collisions?', 'In straight lines at constant speed', ['In circles', 'With constant acceleration downwards', 'They do not move between collisions'], 'With no forces between collisions, each molecule moves in a straight line at constant velocity.')
N('E', 2, 'Calculate the mass of 3.5 mol of oxygen gas, molar mass 0.032 kg mol⁻¹.', 3.5 * 0.032, 'kg', 'm = nM = 3.5 × 0.032 = 0.112 kg', ['C1: m = nM'], aq='m')
M('E', 1, 'Why does a gas fill any container it is placed in?', 'Its molecules move randomly in all directions with little attraction between them', ['Its molecules repel each other strongly', 'Gravity pulls the molecules outwards', 'The container attracts the molecules'], 'Random, nearly free motion spreads the molecules through the whole available volume.')
N('E', 2, 'A balloon contains 0.20 mol of helium (molar mass 4.0 g mol⁻¹). Calculate the mass of the helium in kg.', 0.20 * 0.004, 'kg', 'm = nM = 0.20 × 0.0040 = 8.0 × 10⁻⁴ kg', ['C1: molar mass in kg mol⁻¹'], sig=2, aq='m')
M('E', 1, 'Which observation supports the idea that gas molecules are in constant random motion?', 'A smell spreading across a room even with no draughts', ['A gas has a fixed volume', 'Gases are heavier than liquids', 'Gases cannot be compressed'], 'Diffusion of the scent molecules through the air shows random molecular motion.')
N('E', 2, 'A room contains 2.5 × 10²⁷ air molecules. How many moles of air is this?' + take('NA'), 2.5e27 / NA_, 'mol', f'n = N/N_A = 2.5 × 10²⁷ ÷ 6.02 × 10²³ = {sf(2.5e27 / NA_)} mol', ['C1: n = N/N_A'], aq='n')
M('E', 1, 'What is meant by the statement that collisions between ideal-gas molecules are elastic?', 'Total kinetic energy is conserved in each collision', ['The molecules stick together', 'Momentum is lost', 'The molecules change shape'], 'In an elastic collision no kinetic energy is converted to other forms.')
M('E', 1, 'Why do the smoke particles in a Brownian-motion experiment move more vigorously when the air is warmer?', 'The air molecules move faster, so their impacts are harder and more frequent', ['The smoke particles become lighter', 'There are fewer air molecules', 'Convection currents are stronger'], 'Higher temperature means a higher mean molecular kinetic energy.')
N('E', 2, 'Calculate the number of molecules in 18 g of water (molar mass 18 g mol⁻¹).' + take('NA'), NA_, None, 'n = 18 ÷ 18 = 1.0 mol, so N = 1.0 × 6.02 × 10²³ = 6.02 × 10²³', ['C1: n = m/M then N = nN_A'], aq='N')
M('E', 1, 'Which of these is NOT an assumption of the kinetic theory of an ideal gas?', 'All the molecules move at the same speed', ['The molecules move randomly', 'There are a very large number of molecules', 'The duration of a collision is very short compared with the time between collisions'], 'Molecules have a range of speeds; only the distribution is fixed by the temperature.')
# medium
N('M', 3, 'Calculate the mass of one nitrogen molecule (molar mass 0.028 kg mol⁻¹). Give your answer in units of 10⁻²⁶ kg.' + take('NA'), 0.028 / NA_, '10⁻²⁶ kg', f'm = M/N_A = 0.028 ÷ 6.02 × 10²³ = {sf(0.028 / NA_)} kg', ['C1: m = M/N_A', 'C1: molar mass in kg'], aq='m')
N('M', 3, 'At room conditions a gas has 2.5 × 10²⁵ molecules per m³. Estimate the average spacing between molecules, assuming each occupies a small cube. Give your answer in nm.', (1 / 2.5e25) ** (1 / 3) * 1e9, 'nm', f'Volume per molecule = 1 ÷ 2.5 × 10²⁵ = 4.0 × 10⁻²⁶ m³. Spacing = (4.0 × 10⁻²⁶)^(1/3) = {sf((4e-26) ** (1 / 3))} m. In nm: {sf((4e-26) ** (1 / 3), 4)} × 10⁹ = {sf((4e-26) ** (1 / 3) * 1e9)}', ['C1: volume per molecule', 'C1: cube root', 'C1: convert'], aq='d')
M('M', 3, 'A molecule has a diameter of about 0.3 nm and the mean spacing in air is about 3 nm. Which assumption of the kinetic theory does this support?', 'The volume of the molecules is negligible compared with the volume of the gas', ['Collisions are elastic', 'The molecules move randomly', 'The molecules have a range of speeds'], 'Spacing ≈ 10 × diameter means the molecules occupy only about 1/1000 of the volume.', ['C1: compare sizes', 'C1: negligible volume'])
N('M', 3, 'A sealed flask of volume 2.0 × 10⁻³ m³ contains 0.090 mol of air. Calculate the number of molecules per cubic metre.' + take('NA'), 0.090 * NA_ / 2.0e-3, 'm⁻³', f'N = 0.090 × 6.02 × 10²³ = {sf(0.09 * NA_)}. N/V = {sf(0.09 * NA_, 4)} ÷ 2.0 × 10⁻³ = {sf(0.09 * NA_ / 2e-3)} m⁻³', ['C1: N = nN_A', 'C1: ÷ V'], aq='n')
N('M', 4, 'A helium atom has mass 6.6 × 10⁻²⁷ kg. Calculate the number of atoms in 1.0 g of helium, and hence estimate the Avogadro constant from the molar mass 4.0 g mol⁻¹. Give your estimate of the Avogadro constant.', 0.004 / 6.6e-27, 'mol⁻¹', f'Atoms in 1.0 g = 1.0 × 10⁻³ ÷ 6.6 × 10⁻²⁷ = {sf(1e-3 / 6.6e-27)}. In 4.0 g (one mole): 4.0 × 10⁻³ ÷ 6.6 × 10⁻²⁷ = {sf(0.004 / 6.6e-27)}', ['C1: N = m/m_atom', 'C1: one mole = 4.0 g', 'C1: substitution'], aq='N_A')
M('M', 3, 'In Brownian motion, why are larger smoke particles seen to move less than smaller ones?', 'The random imbalance of impacts is smaller relative to their larger mass and size', ['Larger particles are hit less often', 'Larger particles are colder', 'Larger particles are attracted to the walls'], 'A larger particle receives more impacts, so they average out better, and its larger mass gives a smaller acceleration.', ['C1: impacts average out', 'C1: larger inertia'])
N('M', 3, 'A gas cylinder contains 4.0 kg of propane (molar mass 0.044 kg mol⁻¹). Calculate the number of molecules.' + take('NA'), 4.0 / 0.044 * NA_, None, f'n = m/M = 4.0 ÷ 0.044 = {sf(4 / 0.044)} mol. N = nN_A = {sf(4 / 0.044, 4)} × 6.02 × 10²³ = {sf(4 / 0.044 * NA_)}', ['C1: n = m/M', 'C1: N = nN_A'], aq='N')
M('M', 4, 'Why is the duration of a molecular collision assumed to be much less than the time between collisions?', 'So that at any instant almost all molecules are moving freely, and forces act only during brief impacts', ['So that collisions are inelastic', 'So that the molecules have no speed', 'So that gravity can be ignored'], 'If collisions were long, the molecules would spend significant time interacting, and the simple model (free straight-line motion) would fail.', ['C1: free motion most of the time', 'C1: forces only briefly', 'C1: simplifies the model'])
N('M', 3, 'A scuba tank holds 2.4 × 10²⁶ molecules of air (mean molar mass 0.029 kg mol⁻¹). Calculate the mass of air in the tank.' + take('NA'), 2.4e26 / NA_ * 0.029, 'kg', f'n = 2.4 × 10²⁶ ÷ 6.02 × 10²³ = {sf(2.4e26 / NA_)} mol. m = nM = {sf(2.4e26 / NA_, 4)} × 0.029 = {sf(2.4e26 / NA_ * 0.029)} kg', ['C1: n = N/N_A', 'C1: m = nM'], aq='m')
M('M', 3, 'A gas is compressed so its molecules are closer together. Which kinetic-theory assumption becomes less valid?', 'That the molecules\' own volume and the forces between them are negligible', ['That the molecules move randomly', 'That there are many molecules', 'That the collisions are short'], 'At high density the molecules occupy a larger fraction of the volume and are close enough to attract each other.', ['C1: volume of molecules', 'C1: intermolecular forces'])
N('M', 4, 'Estimate the diameter of an argon atom, assuming that in liquid argon (density 1400 kg m⁻³, molar mass 0.040 kg mol⁻¹) the atoms are packed in cubes. Give your answer in nm.' + take('NA'), (0.040 / (1400 * NA_)) ** (1 / 3) * 1e9, 'nm',
  f'Volume per atom = M/(ρN_A) = 0.040 ÷ (1400 × 6.02 × 10²³) = {sf(0.04 / (1400 * NA_))} m³. d = ({sf(0.04 / (1400 * NA_), 4)})^(1/3) = {sf((0.04 / (1400 * NA_)) ** (1 / 3))} m. In nm: {sf((0.04 / (1400 * NA_)) ** (1 / 3), 4)} × 10⁹ = {sf((0.04 / (1400 * NA_)) ** (1 / 3) * 1e9)}',
  ['C1: volume per atom', 'C1: cube root', 'C1: convert to nm'], aq='d')
M('M', 3, 'Which statement about the speeds of molecules in a gas at constant temperature is correct?', 'The molecules have a wide range of speeds, but the distribution stays the same over time', ['All molecules have the same speed', 'The speeds gradually fall to zero', 'Faster molecules gradually escape until all have the same speed'], 'Collisions constantly exchange energy, but the overall distribution is steady at a given temperature.', ['C1: range of speeds', 'C1: steady distribution'])
N('M', 3, 'A droplet of mercury contains 3.0 × 10¹⁸ atoms. Mercury has molar mass 0.201 kg mol⁻¹. Calculate the mass of the droplet in mg.' + take('NA'), 3.0e18 / NA_ * 0.201 * 1e6, 'mg', f'n = 3.0 × 10¹⁸ ÷ 6.02 × 10²³ = {sf(3e18 / NA_)} mol. m = nM = {sf(3e18 / NA_, 4)} × 0.201 = {sf(3e18 / NA_ * 0.201)} kg. In mg: {sf(3e18 / NA_ * 0.201, 4)} × 10⁶ = {sf(3e18 / NA_ * 0.201 * 1e6)}', ['C1: n = N/N_A', 'C1: m = nM', 'C1: convert'], aq='m')
M('M', 3, 'A student argues: "Gas molecules must slow down when they hit the walls, so the gas gradually cools." Which is the correct response?', 'For an ideal gas the collisions are elastic: the molecules rebound with the same speed, so no kinetic energy is lost', ['The student is right', 'The molecules speed up at the walls', 'The walls absorb the molecules'], 'Elastic collisions conserve kinetic energy; a gas in equilibrium with its container does not cool.', ['C1: elastic collisions', 'C1: no loss of kinetic energy'])
N('M', 4, 'A container of volume 0.025 m³ holds nitrogen (molar mass 0.028 kg mol⁻¹) of density 1.2 kg m⁻³. Calculate the number of molecules.' + take('NA'), 1.2 * 0.025 / 0.028 * NA_, None, f'm = ρV = 1.2 × 0.025 = 0.030 kg. n = 0.030 ÷ 0.028 = {sf(0.03 / 0.028)} mol. N = {sf(0.03 / 0.028, 4)} × 6.02 × 10²³ = {sf(0.03 / 0.028 * NA_)}', ['C1: m = ρV', 'C1: n = m/M', 'C1: N = nN_A'], aq='N')
M('M', 3, 'Why is it reasonable to treat gas molecules as "point particles" when explaining pressure?', 'Their size is tiny compared with the distances they travel between collisions', ['They have no mass', 'They are all identical', 'They never collide with each other'], 'Pressure comes from momentum transfer at the walls; the molecules\' sizes barely matter when they are so far apart.', ['C1: size vs spacing', 'C1: negligible'])
N('M', 3, 'A sample of xenon (molar mass 0.131 kg mol⁻¹) has a mass of 0.65 kg. Calculate the number of moles and the number of atoms. Give the number of atoms.' + take('NA'), 0.65 / 0.131 * NA_, None, f'n = 0.65 ÷ 0.131 = {sf(0.65 / 0.131)} mol. N = {sf(0.65 / 0.131, 4)} × 6.02 × 10²³ = {sf(0.65 / 0.131 * NA_)}', ['C1: n = m/M', 'C1: N = nN_A'], aq='N')
# hard
N('H', 5, 'Air at room temperature has a density of 1.2 kg m⁻³ and a mean molar mass of 0.029 kg mol⁻¹. (a) Calculate the number of molecules per m³. (b) Estimate the mean spacing between molecules. (c) A molecule has diameter 3.5 × 10⁻¹⁰ m. Estimate the fraction of the volume occupied by the molecules themselves. Give only the answer to (b), in nm.' + take('NA'), (1 / (1.2 / 0.029 * NA_)) ** (1 / 3) * 1e9, 'nm',
  f'(a) n = 1.2 ÷ 0.029 × 6.02 × 10²³ = {sf(1.2 / 0.029 * NA_)} m⁻³. (b) spacing = (1 ÷ {sf(1.2 / 0.029 * NA_, 4)})^(1/3) = {sf((1 / (1.2 / 0.029 * NA_)) ** (1 / 3))} m. In nm: {sf((1 / (1.2 / 0.029 * NA_)) ** (1 / 3), 4)} × 10⁹ = {sf((1 / (1.2 / 0.029 * NA_)) ** (1 / 3) * 1e9)}. (c) fraction ≈ n × (π/6)d³ ≈ {sf(1.2 / 0.029 * NA_ * pi / 6 * (3.5e-10) ** 3)}: about 0.06 %.',
  ['C1: number density', 'A1: n', 'C1: cube root of volume per molecule', 'A1: spacing', 'B1: fraction small'], aq='d')
N('H', 6, 'A Brownian-motion experiment uses smoke particles of mass 1.0 × 10⁻¹⁶ kg in air at 300 K. Each particle has the same mean kinetic energy as an air molecule, (3/2)kT. (a) Calculate this mean kinetic energy, in units of 10⁻²¹ J. (b) Calculate the root-mean-square speed of a smoke particle. (c) Explain why the smoke particle moves so much more slowly than an air molecule (rms speed about 500 m s⁻¹). Give only the answer to (b), in mm s⁻¹.' + take('k'), sqrt(3 * k_ * 300 / 1.0e-16) * 1000, 'mm s⁻¹',
  f'(a) ⟨Ek⟩ = 1.5 × 1.38 × 10⁻²³ × 300 = {sf(1.5 * k_ * 300)} J = {sf(1.5 * k_ * 300 / 1e-21)} × 10⁻²¹ J. (b) c_rms = √(3kT/m) = √(3 × 1.38 × 10⁻²³ × 300 ÷ 1.0 × 10⁻¹⁶) = {sf(sqrt(3 * k_ * 300 / 1e-16))} m s⁻¹. In mm s⁻¹: {sf(sqrt(3 * k_ * 300 / 1e-16), 4)} × 1000 = {sf(sqrt(3 * k_ * 300 / 1e-16) * 1000)}. (c) Same kinetic energy but about 10¹⁰ times the mass, so a speed about 10⁵ times smaller.',
  ['C1: (3/2)kT', 'A1: energy', 'C1: c_rms = √(3kT/m)', 'A1: c_rms', 'B1: much larger mass', 'B1: same mean Ek'], aq='c_rms')
N('H', 5, 'A cylinder of volume 0.050 m³ contains 2.4 mol of an ideal gas. (a) Calculate the number of molecules. (b) Calculate the number density. (c) The volume is halved at constant temperature. State and explain the effect on the rate at which molecules hit each square metre of wall. Give only the answer to (b).' + take('NA'), 2.4 * NA_ / 0.050, 'm⁻³',
  f'(a) N = 2.4 × 6.02 × 10²³ = {sf(2.4 * NA_)}. (b) n = N/V = {sf(2.4 * NA_, 4)} ÷ 0.050 = {sf(2.4 * NA_ / 0.05)} m⁻³. (c) The number density doubles at the same molecular speeds, so the rate of collisions per m² doubles (and so does the pressure).',
  ['A1: N', 'C1: n = N/V', 'A1: n', 'B1: rate doubles', 'B1: same speed, twice density'], aq='n')
N('H', 6, 'A diver\'s lungs hold 6.0 litres of air at the surface (1.0 × 10⁵ Pa, 300 K), containing about 0.24 mol. (a) Calculate the number of molecules. (b) The air is 21 % oxygen by number. Calculate the mass of oxygen (molar mass 0.032 kg mol⁻¹). (c) At 20 m depth the pressure is 3.0 × 10⁵ Pa. Explain, in terms of molecules, why the same lung volume now holds three times as much air. Give only the answer to (b).' + take('NA'), 0.21 * 0.24 * 0.032, 'kg',
  f'(a) N = 0.24 × 6.02 × 10²³ = {sf(0.24 * NA_)}. (b) n(O₂) = 0.21 × 0.24 = {sf(0.0504)} mol, m = {sf(0.0504, 4)} × 0.032 = {sf(0.0504 * 0.032)} kg. (c) Three times the pressure at the same temperature needs three times the number of molecules per unit volume hitting the walls with the same mean speed.',
  ['A1: N', 'C1: moles of O₂', 'C1: m = nM', 'A1: m', 'B1: n ∝ p at same T', 'B1: molecular explanation'], aq='m')
N('H', 5, 'Interstellar space contains about 1.0 × 10⁶ hydrogen atoms per m³ at about 100 K. (a) Calculate the mass of hydrogen in 1.0 m³ (atom mass 1.67 × 10⁻²⁷ kg), in units of 10⁻²¹ kg. (b) Estimate the mean spacing between atoms. (c) Explain why the gas still exerts a pressure. Give only the answer to (b), in mm.', (1 / 1.0e6) ** (1 / 3) * 1000, 'mm',
  f'(a) m = 1.0 × 10⁶ × 1.67 × 10⁻²⁷ = 1.67 × 10⁻²¹ kg = 1.67 × 10⁻²¹ kg (1.67 in units of 10⁻²¹ kg). (b) spacing = (1 ÷ 1.0 × 10⁶)^(1/3) = 0.010 m. In mm: 0.010 × 1000 = 10. (c) The atoms still move randomly and collide with any surface, transferring momentum.',
  ['C1: m = n × m_atom', 'A1: mass', 'C1: cube root', 'A1: spacing', 'B1: molecular impacts'], sig=2, aq='d')

# ═════════════════════════ 20.2 Explaining pressure (16/18/10) ═════════════════════════
lesson('20.2')
M('E', 1, 'How does the kinetic model explain the pressure of a gas on its container?', 'Molecules collide with the walls and change momentum; the walls exert forces on them and they exert equal forces on the walls', ['Molecules are attracted to the walls', 'The weight of the gas presses on the walls', 'The walls push the molecules outwards'], 'Each collision gives a change of momentum; the rate of change of momentum per unit area is the pressure.')
N('E', 2, 'A gas exerts a pressure of 1.2 × 10⁵ Pa on a piston of area 0.015 m². Calculate the force on the piston.', 1.2e5 * 0.015, 'N', 'F = pA = 1.2 × 10⁵ × 0.015 = 1800 N', ['C1: F = pA'], sig=2, aq='F')
N('E', 2, 'A force of 650 N acts uniformly on a valve of area 4.0 × 10⁻³ m². Calculate the pressure.', 650 / 4.0e-3, 'Pa', 'p = F/A = 650 ÷ 4.0 × 10⁻³ = 1.6 × 10⁵ Pa', ['C1: p = F/A'], sig=2, aq='p')
M('E', 1, 'A molecule of mass m moving at speed u hits a wall at right angles and rebounds elastically. What is the magnitude of its change of momentum?', '2mu', ['mu', 'zero', 'mu/2'], 'Momentum changes from +mu to −mu: a change of 2mu.')
N('E', 2, 'A nitrogen molecule (mass 4.65 × 10⁻²⁶ kg) moving at 500 m s⁻¹ rebounds elastically at right angles from a wall. Calculate its change in momentum, in units of 10⁻²³ kg m s⁻¹.', 2 * 4.65e-26 * 500, '10⁻²³ kg m s⁻¹', f'Δp = 2mu = 2 × 4.65 × 10⁻²⁶ × 500 = {sf(2 * 4.65e-26 * 500)} kg m s⁻¹', ['C1: Δp = 2mu'], aq='Δp')
M('E', 1, 'A gas in a sealed rigid container is heated. Why does its pressure rise?', 'The molecules move faster, so they hit the walls harder and more often', ['More molecules are created', 'The molecules expand', 'The container shrinks'], 'Higher temperature means higher molecular speeds: more momentum change per collision and more collisions per second.')
M('E', 1, 'The number of molecules in a container is doubled at constant temperature and volume. What happens to the pressure?', 'It doubles', ['It halves', 'It stays the same', 'It quadruples'], 'Twice as many molecules hit the walls per second with the same speeds, so the pressure doubles.')
M('E', 1, 'Which equation relates the pressure of an ideal gas to the mean square speed of its molecules?', 'pV = ⅓Nm⟨c²⟩', ['pV = Nm⟨c²⟩', 'pV = ½Nm⟨c⟩²', 'p = Nm⟨c²⟩V'], 'Derived from Newton\'s laws applied to molecular collisions: pV = ⅓Nm⟨c²⟩.')
N('E', 2, 'A gas has density 1.8 kg m⁻³ and mean square speed 2.5 × 10⁵ m² s⁻². Calculate its pressure using p = ⅓ρ⟨c²⟩.', 1.8 * 2.5e5 / 3, 'Pa', 'p = ⅓ρ⟨c²⟩ = 1.8 × 2.5 × 10⁵ ÷ 3 = 1.5 × 10⁵ Pa', ['C1: p = ⅓ρ⟨c²⟩'], sig=2, aq='p')
M('E', 1, 'Why does the factor ⅓ appear in pV = ⅓Nm⟨c²⟩?', 'Molecules move in three dimensions; on average one third of ⟨c²⟩ is in the direction perpendicular to a given wall', ['Only a third of the molecules hit the walls', 'A third of the energy is lost', 'The container has three pairs of walls'], '⟨c²⟩ = ⟨cₓ²⟩ + ⟨c_y²⟩ + ⟨c_z²⟩ and, by symmetry, each is ⅓⟨c²⟩.')
N('E', 2, 'Calculate the force exerted by atmospheric pressure (1.0 × 10⁵ Pa) on a window of area 1.2 m².', 1.0e5 * 1.2, 'N', 'F = pA = 1.0 × 10⁵ × 1.2 = 1.2 × 10⁵ N', ['C1: F = pA'], sig=2, aq='F')
M('E', 1, 'Why doesn\'t the large force of the atmosphere on a window break it?', 'Air pushes with an almost equal force on the other side', ['Air has no mass', 'Glass is stronger than any force', 'Atmospheric pressure acts only downwards'], 'The pressures on both sides are about equal, so the resultant force is nearly zero.')
N('E', 2, 'A gas has pressure 2.0 × 10⁵ Pa and density 2.4 kg m⁻³. Calculate the mean square speed of its molecules.', 3 * 2.0e5 / 2.4, 'm² s⁻²', '⟨c²⟩ = 3p/ρ = 3 × 2.0 × 10⁵ ÷ 2.4 = 2.5 × 10⁵ m² s⁻²', ['C1: rearranged p = ⅓ρ⟨c²⟩'], sig=2, aq='⟨c²⟩')
M('E', 1, 'A balloon is squeezed to half its volume at constant temperature. Why does the pressure double?', 'The same molecules hit the smaller wall area twice as often', ['The molecules move twice as fast', 'The number of molecules doubles', 'The molecules become heavier'], 'At constant T the speeds are unchanged; halving V doubles the number density and so the collision rate per unit area.')
N('E', 2, 'Calculate the root-mean-square speed of molecules whose mean square speed is 1.6 × 10⁵ m² s⁻².', sqrt(1.6e5), 'm s⁻¹', f'c_rms = √⟨c²⟩ = √(1.6 × 10⁵) = {sf(sqrt(1.6e5))} m s⁻¹', ['C1: square root'], aq='c_rms')
M('E', 1, 'Which quantity is the root-mean-square speed of gas molecules?', 'The square root of the average of the squares of the speeds', ['The average of the speeds', 'The most common speed', 'The square of the average speed'], 'c_rms = √⟨c²⟩: square each speed, average, then take the square root.')
# medium
spd = [200, 400, 600]
N('M', 3, 'Three molecules have speeds 200, 400 and 600 m s⁻¹. Calculate their mean square speed and their root-mean-square speed. Give the r.m.s. speed.', sqrt((200 ** 2 + 400 ** 2 + 600 ** 2) / 3), 'm s⁻¹', f'⟨c²⟩ = (200² + 400² + 600²) ÷ 3 = {sf((200 ** 2 + 400 ** 2 + 600 ** 2) / 3)} m² s⁻². c_rms = √{sf((200 ** 2 + 400 ** 2 + 600 ** 2) / 3, 4)} = {sf(sqrt((200 ** 2 + 400 ** 2 + 600 ** 2) / 3))} m s⁻¹ (the mean speed is 400 m s⁻¹).', ['C1: squares averaged', 'C1: square root', 'B1: differs from mean speed'], aq='c_rms')
N('M', 4, 'A container of volume 0.040 m³ holds 1.5 × 10²⁴ molecules, each of mass 5.3 × 10⁻²⁶ kg, with r.m.s. speed 480 m s⁻¹. Calculate the pressure.', 1.5e24 * 5.3e-26 * 480 ** 2 / (3 * 0.040), 'Pa', f'p = Nm⟨c²⟩/(3V) = 1.5 × 10²⁴ × 5.3 × 10⁻²⁶ × 480² ÷ (3 × 0.040) = {sf(1.5e24 * 5.3e-26 * 480 ** 2 / 0.12)} Pa', ['C1: pV = ⅓Nm⟨c²⟩', 'C1: ⟨c²⟩ = c_rms²', 'C1: substitution'], aq='p')
M('M', 3, 'In deriving pV = ⅓Nm⟨c²⟩, a molecule moving at speed u between two walls a distance L apart hits one wall how often?', 'Once every 2L/u seconds', ['Once every L/u seconds', 'Once every L/2u seconds', 'Once every u/L seconds'], 'It travels to the far wall and back (2L) between successive hits on the same wall.', ['C1: round trip 2L', 'C1: time = 2L/u'])
N('M', 3, 'A molecule of mass 4.8 × 10⁻²⁶ kg bounces back and forth between two walls 0.20 m apart at 450 m s⁻¹. Calculate the average force it exerts on one wall, in units of 10⁻²³ N.', 4.8e-26 * 450 ** 2 / 0.20, '10⁻²³ N', f'Δp per hit = 2mu, hits per second = u/2L, so F = mu²/L = 4.8 × 10⁻²⁶ × 450² ÷ 0.20 = {sf(4.8e-26 * 450 ** 2 / 0.2)} N', ['C1: Δp = 2mu', 'C1: rate u/2L', 'C1: F = mu²/L'], aq='F')
M('M', 4, 'Gas A has molecules of mass m and gas B has molecules of mass 4m. They have the same pressure, volume and number of molecules. Which is correct?', 'The r.m.s. speed in A is twice that in B', ['The r.m.s. speed in B is twice that in A', 'The r.m.s. speeds are equal', 'The r.m.s. speed in A is four times that in B'], 'pV = ⅓Nm⟨c²⟩: with pV and N equal, m⟨c²⟩ is equal, so ⟨c²⟩ ∝ 1/m and c_rms ∝ 1/√m.', ['C1: m⟨c²⟩ constant', 'C1: c_rms ∝ 1/√m', 'C1: factor 2'])
N('M', 3, 'At sea level air has pressure 1.01 × 10⁵ Pa and density 1.29 kg m⁻³. Calculate the r.m.s. speed of the air molecules.', sqrt(3 * 1.01e5 / 1.29), 'm s⁻¹', f'c_rms = √(3p/ρ) = √(3 × 1.01 × 10⁵ ÷ 1.29) = {sf(sqrt(3 * 1.01e5 / 1.29))} m s⁻¹', ['C1: p = ⅓ρ⟨c²⟩', 'C1: rearranged', 'C1: square root'], aq='c_rms')
M('M', 3, 'A gas is heated at constant volume until the r.m.s. speed of its molecules doubles. What happens to its pressure?', 'It increases by a factor of 4', ['It doubles', 'It stays the same', 'It increases by a factor of √2'], 'p ∝ ⟨c²⟩ at constant V and N: doubling c_rms quadruples ⟨c²⟩.', ['C1: p ∝ ⟨c²⟩', 'C1: factor 4'])
N('M', 4, 'Five molecules have speeds 300, 350, 400, 450, 500 m s⁻¹. Calculate (a) the mean speed and (b) the r.m.s. speed. Give only the answer to (b).', sqrt(sum(v * v for v in [300, 350, 400, 450, 500]) / 5), 'm s⁻¹', f'(a) mean = 2000 ÷ 5 = 400 m s⁻¹. (b) ⟨c²⟩ = (300² + 350² + 400² + 450² + 500²) ÷ 5 = {sf(sum(v * v for v in [300, 350, 400, 450, 500]) / 5)} m² s⁻². c_rms = √{sf(sum(v * v for v in [300, 350, 400, 450, 500]) / 5, 4)} = {sf(sqrt(sum(v * v for v in [300, 350, 400, 450, 500]) / 5))} m s⁻¹', ['B1: mean speed', 'C1: mean square', 'C1: square root'], aq='c_rms')
M('M', 3, 'Why does a gas exert the same pressure in all directions?', 'The molecules move randomly, so on average equal numbers move in every direction', ['Gravity acts in all directions', 'The walls push equally', 'The molecules are at rest'], 'Random motion means no preferred direction, so the momentum transfer per unit area is the same on every wall.', ['C1: random motion', 'C1: no preferred direction'])
N('M', 3, 'A gas at 1.5 × 10⁵ Pa has molecules with r.m.s. speed 520 m s⁻¹. Calculate its density.', 3 * 1.5e5 / 520 ** 2, 'kg m⁻³', f'ρ = 3p/⟨c²⟩ = 3 × 1.5 × 10⁵ ÷ 520² = {sf(4.5e5 / 520 ** 2)} kg m⁻³', ['C1: p = ⅓ρ⟨c²⟩', 'C1: rearranged'], aq='ρ')
M('M', 3, 'A tyre pump compresses air to half its volume quickly, and the temperature rises. Which statement explains why the pressure more than doubles?', 'The number density doubles AND the molecules move faster', ['Only the number density doubles', 'Only the speeds increase', 'The molecules become larger'], 'Both factors in p = ⅓(N/V)m⟨c²⟩ increase.', ['C1: N/V doubles', 'C1: ⟨c²⟩ increases'])
N('M', 4, 'A cubic box of side 0.10 m contains 3.0 × 10²² helium atoms (mass 6.6 × 10⁻²⁷ kg) with r.m.s. speed 1.3 × 10³ m s⁻¹. Calculate the force on one face of the box.', 3.0e22 * 6.6e-27 * 1.3e3 ** 2 / (3 * 0.10 ** 3) * 0.10 ** 2, 'N', f'p = Nm⟨c²⟩/3V = 3.0 × 10²² × 6.6 × 10⁻²⁷ × (1.3 × 10³)² ÷ (3 × 1.0 × 10⁻³) = {sf(3e22 * 6.6e-27 * 1.69e6 / 3e-3)} Pa. F = pA = {sf(3e22 * 6.6e-27 * 1.69e6 / 3e-3, 4)} × 0.010 = {sf(3e22 * 6.6e-27 * 1.69e6 / 3e-3 * 0.01)} N', ['C1: V = L³', 'C1: p from kinetic formula', 'C1: F = pA'], aq='F')
M('M', 3, 'In the derivation of pV = ⅓Nm⟨c²⟩, collisions between molecules are ignored. Why does this not affect the result?', 'Elastic collisions between molecules just exchange momentum; the average momentum carried to the walls is unchanged', ['There are no collisions between molecules', 'Molecules stick together when they collide', 'The walls absorb all the energy'], 'Momentum and kinetic energy are conserved in molecular collisions, so the total rate of momentum transfer to the walls is unchanged.', ['C1: elastic collisions', 'C1: momentum conserved overall'])
N('M', 3, 'Calculate the total kinetic energy of the molecules in 0.020 m³ of a gas at 1.0 × 10⁵ Pa, using pV = ⅓Nm⟨c²⟩ and Ek = ½Nm⟨c²⟩.', 1.5 * 1.0e5 * 0.020, 'J', 'Nm⟨c²⟩ = 3pV, so total Ek = ½ × 3pV = 1.5 × 1.0 × 10⁵ × 0.020 = 3000 J', ['C1: Nm⟨c²⟩ = 3pV', 'C1: Ek = (3/2)pV'], sig=2, aq='Ek')
M('M', 4, 'A gas is compressed isothermally to one third of its volume. Which row is correct?', 'Pressure ×3; r.m.s. speed unchanged; collision rate per unit area ×3', ['Pressure ×3; r.m.s. speed ×3; collision rate unchanged', 'Pressure ×9; r.m.s. speed ×3; collision rate ×3', 'Pressure unchanged; r.m.s. speed unchanged; collision rate ×3'], 'Isothermal: speeds unchanged. Number density ×3 so collisions per unit area ×3, and p ×3.', ['C1: same T, same speeds', 'C1: density ×3', 'C1: p ×3'])
N('M', 3, 'The r.m.s. speed of hydrogen molecules in a sample is 1900 m s⁻¹ at a pressure of 1.0 × 10⁵ Pa. Calculate the density of the gas.', 3 * 1.0e5 / 1900 ** 2, 'kg m⁻³', f'ρ = 3p/c_rms² = 3 × 1.0 × 10⁵ ÷ 1900² = {sf(3e5 / 1900 ** 2)} kg m⁻³', ['C1: p = ⅓ρ⟨c²⟩', 'C1: substitution'], aq='ρ')
# hard
N('H', 6, 'A molecule of mass m moves in a cubical box of side L with velocity components (u, v, w). (a) Show that the force it exerts on the wall perpendicular to the x-direction is mu²/L. (b) Hence show that the pressure due to N molecules is p = Nm⟨u²⟩/L³. (c) Explain how this leads to pV = ⅓Nm⟨c²⟩. A box of side 0.30 m contains 6.0 × 10²³ molecules of mass 4.8 × 10⁻²⁶ kg with ⟨c²⟩ = 2.4 × 10⁵ m² s⁻². Give only the pressure.', 6.0e23 * 4.8e-26 * 2.4e5 / (3 * 0.30 ** 3), 'Pa',
  f'(a) Δp = 2mu per hit; time between hits = 2L/u; F = 2mu ÷ (2L/u) = mu²/L. (b) Sum over N molecules: F = Nm⟨u²⟩/L, p = F/L² = Nm⟨u²⟩/L³. (c) Random motion: ⟨u²⟩ = ⅓⟨c²⟩ and L³ = V, so pV = ⅓Nm⟨c²⟩. p = 6.0 × 10²³ × 4.8 × 10⁻²⁶ × 2.4 × 10⁵ ÷ (3 × 0.30³) = {sf(6e23 * 4.8e-26 * 2.4e5 / (3 * 0.027))} Pa',
  ['M1: Δp = 2mu', 'M1: time between hits 2L/u', 'A1: F = mu²/L', 'M1: ⟨u²⟩ = ⅓⟨c²⟩', 'C1: substitution', 'A1: p'], aq='p')
N('H', 5, 'A gas cylinder of volume 0.050 m³ contains oxygen at 2.0 × 10⁶ Pa. The molecules have r.m.s. speed 480 m s⁻¹. (a) Calculate the density of the oxygen. (b) Calculate the mass of oxygen. (c) Some gas is released at constant temperature until the pressure is 1.0 × 10⁶ Pa. Explain the effect on the r.m.s. speed and the density. Give only the answer to (b).', 3 * 2.0e6 / 480 ** 2 * 0.050, 'kg',
  f'(a) ρ = 3p/⟨c²⟩ = 3 × 2.0 × 10⁶ ÷ 480² = {sf(6e6 / 480 ** 2)} kg m⁻³. (b) m = ρV = {sf(6e6 / 480 ** 2, 4)} × 0.050 = {sf(6e6 / 480 ** 2 * 0.05)} kg. (c) Same temperature, so the r.m.s. speed is unchanged; the density halves.',
  ['C1: ρ = 3p/⟨c²⟩', 'A1: ρ', 'C1: m = ρV', 'A1: m', 'B1: speed unchanged, density halves'], aq='m')
N('H', 6, 'In a beam experiment, 2.0 × 10²² nitrogen molecules per second (mass 4.65 × 10⁻²⁶ kg each), all moving at 500 m s⁻¹, strike a plate at right angles and rebound elastically. (a) Calculate the momentum change of one molecule. (b) Calculate the force on the plate. (c) The plate has area 4.0 cm². Calculate the pressure, and explain why a real gas pressure is a third of what this simple beam picture suggests. Give only the force in (b).', 2.0e22 * 2 * 4.65e-26 * 500, 'N',
  f'(a) Δp = 2mu = 2 × 4.65 × 10⁻²⁶ × 500 = {sf(2 * 4.65e-26 * 500)} kg m s⁻¹. (b) F = rate × Δp = 2.0 × 10²² × {sf(2 * 4.65e-26 * 500, 4)} = {sf(2e22 * 2 * 4.65e-26 * 500)} N. (c) p = F/A = {sf(2e22 * 2 * 4.65e-26 * 500, 4)} ÷ 4.0 × 10⁻⁴ = {sf(2e22 * 2 * 4.65e-26 * 500 / 4e-4)} Pa. In a gas only the x-component of random velocities hits a wall: ⟨u²⟩ = ⅓⟨c²⟩.',
  ['C1: Δp = 2mu', 'A1: Δp', 'C1: F = (N/t)Δp', 'A1: F', 'C1: p = F/A', 'B1: random directions'], aq='F')
N('H', 5, 'Air in a sealed container is at 1.0 × 10⁵ Pa with r.m.s. speed 500 m s⁻¹. (a) Calculate the density. (b) The container is heated until the r.m.s. speed is 600 m s⁻¹. Calculate the new pressure. (c) Calculate the ratio of new to old absolute temperatures. Give only the answer to (b).', 1.0e5 * (600 / 500) ** 2, 'Pa',
  f'(a) ρ = 3p/⟨c²⟩ = 3 × 1.0 × 10⁵ ÷ 500² = 1.2 kg m⁻³. (b) p ∝ ⟨c²⟩ at constant density: p = 1.0 × 10⁵ × (600 ÷ 500)² = {sf(1e5 * 1.44)} Pa. (c) T ∝ ⟨c²⟩: ratio = 1.44.',
  ['C1: ρ = 3p/⟨c²⟩', 'A1: ρ', 'C1: p ∝ ⟨c²⟩', 'A1: p', 'B1: T ratio 1.44'], aq='p')
N('H', 6, 'A spacecraft cabin of volume 25 m³ is filled with air (density 1.2 kg m⁻³, r.m.s. speed 500 m s⁻¹). (a) Calculate the pressure in the cabin. (b) A 1.0 cm² hole opens. Estimate the force pushing air out through it. (c) Using the kinetic model, explain why the cabin pressure falls only gradually. Give only the answer to (a).', 1.2 * 500 ** 2 / 3, 'Pa',
  f'(a) p = ⅓ρ⟨c²⟩ = 1.2 × 500² ÷ 3 = {sf(1.2 * 250000 / 3)} Pa. (b) F = pA = {sf(1e5)} × 1.0 × 10⁻⁴ = 10 N. (c) Only molecules that happen to reach the hole escape; the number escaping per second is a small fraction of the huge number in the cabin.',
  ['C1: p = ⅓ρ⟨c²⟩', 'C1: substitution', 'A1: p', 'C1: F = pA', 'B1: escape rate small'], aq='p')

# ═════════════════════════ 20.3 The mole and the Avogadro constant (14/17/11) ═════════════════════════
lesson('20.3')
N('E', 1, 'How many moles are there in 88 g of carbon dioxide (molar mass 44 g mol⁻¹)?', 2.0, 'mol', 'n = m/M = 88 ÷ 44 = 2.0 mol', [], sig=2, aq='n')
N('E', 2, 'Calculate the number of atoms in 0.40 mol of neon.' + take('NA'), 0.40 * NA_, None, 'N = nN_A = 0.40 × 6.02 × 10²³ = 2.41 × 10²³', ['C1: N = nN_A'], aq='N')
M('E', 1, 'What is the Avogadro constant?', 'The number of particles in one mole of a substance', ['The mass of one mole of a substance', 'The volume of one mole of gas', 'The number of moles in 1 kg'], 'N_A = 6.02 × 10²³ mol⁻¹: the number of atoms in exactly 12 g of carbon-12.')
M('E', 1, 'One mole of a substance is the amount that contains…', 'as many particles as there are atoms in 12 g of carbon-12', ['1 g of particles', '22.4 litres of particles', '1000 particles'], 'This is the definition of the mole used in the 9702 course.')
N('E', 2, 'Calculate the mass, in kg, of 2.5 mol of sodium chloride (molar mass 0.0585 kg mol⁻¹).', 2.5 * 0.0585, 'kg', 'm = nM = 2.5 × 0.0585 = 0.146 kg', ['C1: m = nM'], aq='m')
M('E', 1, 'Which unit is the SI base unit for amount of substance?', 'mole (mol)', ['kilogram (kg)', 'gram (g)', 'kelvin (K)'], 'The mole is one of the seven SI base units.')
N('E', 2, 'A sample contains 1.2 × 10²⁴ atoms of iron. How many moles is this?' + take('NA'), 1.2e24 / NA_, 'mol', f'n = N/N_A = 1.2 × 10²⁴ ÷ 6.02 × 10²³ = {sf(1.2e24 / NA_)} mol', ['C1: n = N/N_A'], aq='n')
N('E', 2, 'Calculate the number of molecules in 1.0 kg of water (molar mass 0.018 kg mol⁻¹).' + take('NA'), 1.0 / 0.018 * NA_, None, f'n = 1.0 ÷ 0.018 = {sf(1 / 0.018)} mol, N = {sf(1 / 0.018, 4)} × 6.02 × 10²³ = {sf(NA_ / 0.018)}', ['C1: n = m/M, N = nN_A'], aq='N')
M('E', 1, 'One mole of helium (4 g) and one mole of oxygen (32 g). Which is the same for both?', 'The number of molecules', ['The mass', 'The molar mass', 'The density'], 'A mole of any substance contains N_A particles.')
N('E', 2, 'How many moles of copper are in a 0.25 kg block? Molar mass of copper = 0.0635 kg mol⁻¹.', 0.25 / 0.0635, 'mol', f'n = m/M = 0.25 ÷ 0.0635 = {sf(0.25 / 0.0635)} mol', ['C1: n = m/M'], aq='n')
M('E', 1, 'What is the molar mass of a substance?', 'The mass of one mole of it', ['The mass of one molecule', 'The number of molecules per kg', 'The mass per unit volume'], 'Molar mass M = m/n, in kg mol⁻¹ (or g mol⁻¹).')
N('E', 2, 'Calculate the mass of one carbon-12 atom, in units of 10⁻²⁶ kg.' + take('NA'), 0.012 / NA_, '10⁻²⁶ kg', f'm = M/N_A = 0.012 ÷ 6.02 × 10²³ = {sf(0.012 / NA_)} kg', ['C1: m = M/N_A'], aq='m')
N('E', 2, 'A balloon contains 3.0 × 10²³ molecules of helium. Calculate the number of moles.' + take('NA'), 3.0e23 / NA_, 'mol', f'n = 3.0 × 10²³ ÷ 6.02 × 10²³ = {sf(3e23 / NA_)} mol', ['C1: n = N/N_A'], aq='n')
N('E', 2, 'Calculate the mass of 1.5 × 10²⁵ molecules of methane (molar mass 0.016 kg mol⁻¹).' + take('NA'), 1.5e25 / NA_ * 0.016, 'kg', f'n = 1.5 × 10²⁵ ÷ 6.02 × 10²³ = {sf(1.5e25 / NA_)} mol, m = nM = {sf(1.5e25 / NA_, 4)} × 0.016 = {sf(1.5e25 / NA_ * 0.016)} kg', ['C1: n then m = nM'], aq='m')
# medium
N('M', 3, 'A gold ring has mass 6.0 g. Gold has molar mass 0.197 kg mol⁻¹. Calculate the number of gold atoms in the ring.' + take('NA'), 0.006 / 0.197 * NA_, None, f'n = 0.0060 ÷ 0.197 = {sf(0.006 / 0.197)} mol. N = {sf(0.006 / 0.197, 4)} × 6.02 × 10²³ = {sf(0.006 / 0.197 * NA_)}', ['C1: mass in kg', 'C1: n = m/M', 'C1: N = nN_A'], aq='N')
N('M', 3, 'A drop of water has a volume of 0.050 cm³. Water has density 1000 kg m⁻³ and molar mass 0.018 kg mol⁻¹. Calculate the number of molecules in the drop.' + take('NA'), 1000 * 0.050e-6 / 0.018 * NA_, None, f'm = ρV = 1000 × 0.050 × 10⁻⁶ = 5.0 × 10⁻⁵ kg. n = 5.0 × 10⁻⁵ ÷ 0.018 = {sf(5e-5 / 0.018)} mol. N = {sf(5e-5 / 0.018, 4)} × 6.02 × 10²³ = {sf(5e-5 / 0.018 * NA_)}', ['C1: m = ρV with cm³ → m³', 'C1: n = m/M', 'C1: N = nN_A'], aq='N')
M('M', 3, 'Which statement about one mole of hydrogen gas (H₂) is correct?', 'It contains 6.02 × 10²³ molecules and 1.20 × 10²⁴ atoms', ['It contains 6.02 × 10²³ atoms', 'It has a mass of 1 g', 'It contains 3.01 × 10²³ molecules'], 'Each H₂ molecule has 2 atoms; one mole of molecules has mass 2 g.', ['C1: N_A molecules', 'C1: 2 atoms per molecule'])
N('M', 4, 'Aluminium has density 2700 kg m⁻³ and molar mass 0.027 kg mol⁻¹. Calculate the number of atoms in a 1.0 cm³ cube of aluminium.' + take('NA'), 2700 * 1e-6 / 0.027 * NA_, None, f'm = 2700 × 1.0 × 10⁻⁶ = 2.7 × 10⁻³ kg. n = 2.7 × 10⁻³ ÷ 0.027 = 0.10 mol. N = 0.10 × 6.02 × 10²³ = {sf(0.1 * NA_)}', ['C1: m = ρV', 'C1: n = m/M', 'C1: N = nN_A'], aq='N')
N('M', 3, 'Calculate the molar mass of a gas, given that 3.0 × 10²⁴ of its molecules have a mass of 0.14 kg.' + take('NA'), 0.14 / (3.0e24 / NA_), 'kg mol⁻¹', f'n = 3.0 × 10²⁴ ÷ 6.02 × 10²³ = {sf(3e24 / NA_)} mol. M = m/n = 0.14 ÷ {sf(3e24 / NA_, 4)} = {sf(0.14 / (3e24 / NA_))} kg mol⁻¹ (probably nitrogen)', ['C1: n = N/N_A', 'C1: M = m/n'], aq='M')
M('M', 3, 'Why is the Avogadro constant needed to connect the macroscopic gas equation pV = nRT to the microscopic pV = NkT?', 'Because N = nN_A and k = R/N_A, so nR = Nk', ['Because R = kN_A²', 'Because k and R are equal', 'Because moles and molecules are the same'], 'R is per mole, k is per molecule; N_A converts between them.', ['C1: N = nN_A', 'C1: R = N_A k'])
N('M', 3, 'Use R = 8.31 J K⁻¹ mol⁻¹ and k = 1.38 × 10⁻²³ J K⁻¹ to calculate the Avogadro constant.', 8.31 / 1.38e-23, 'mol⁻¹', f'N_A = R/k = 8.31 ÷ 1.38 × 10⁻²³ = {sf(8.31 / 1.38e-23)} mol⁻¹', ['C1: R = N_A k', 'C1: rearranged'], aq='N_A')
N('M', 4, 'A uranium-235 nucleus has mass 235 u. Calculate the number of nuclei in 1.0 kg of uranium-235, using 1 u = 1.66 × 10⁻²⁷ kg, and compare with 1.0 kg ÷ 0.235 kg mol⁻¹ × N_A. Give the number of nuclei.', 1.0 / (235 * 1.66e-27), None, f'mass per nucleus = 235 × 1.66 × 10⁻²⁷ = {sf(235 * 1.66e-27)} kg. N = 1.0 ÷ {sf(235 * 1.66e-27, 4)} = {sf(1 / (235 * 1.66e-27))}. The molar method agrees: 1.0 ÷ 0.235 × 6.02 × 10²³ = {sf(1 / 0.235 * NA_)}.', ['C1: mass per nucleus', 'C1: N = m/m₁', 'B1: comparison'], aq='N')
M('M', 3, 'Which sample contains the greatest number of atoms?', '1.0 g of hydrogen gas (H₂)', ['1.0 g of helium', '1.0 g of oxygen gas', '1.0 g of carbon'], 'H₂: 0.5 mol of molecules = 1.0 mol of atoms. He: 0.25 mol. O₂: 1/32 mol molecules = 1/16 mol atoms. C: 1/12 mol.', ['C1: moles of atoms in each', 'C1: hydrogen largest'])
N('M', 3, 'A sugar cube (sucrose, molar mass 0.342 kg mol⁻¹) has mass 4.0 g. Calculate the number of sucrose molecules.' + take('NA'), 0.004 / 0.342 * NA_, None, f'n = 0.0040 ÷ 0.342 = {sf(0.004 / 0.342)} mol. N = {sf(0.004 / 0.342, 4)} × 6.02 × 10²³ = {sf(0.004 / 0.342 * NA_)}', ['C1: n = m/M', 'C1: N = nN_A'], aq='N')
N('M', 4, 'A thin film of oil (molar mass 0.28 kg mol⁻¹, density 900 kg m⁻³) is one molecule thick. A drop of volume 1.0 × 10⁻¹⁰ m³ spreads to an area of 0.050 m². Estimate the size of a molecule, and the number of molecules in the drop. Give the number of molecules.' + take('NA'), 900 * 1.0e-10 / 0.28 * NA_, None, f'Thickness = V/A = 1.0 × 10⁻¹⁰ ÷ 0.050 = 2.0 × 10⁻⁹ m (molecule size). m = 900 × 1.0 × 10⁻¹⁰ = 9.0 × 10⁻⁸ kg, n = 9.0 × 10⁻⁸ ÷ 0.28 = {sf(9e-8 / 0.28)} mol, N = {sf(9e-8 / 0.28, 4)} × 6.02 × 10²³ = {sf(9e-8 / 0.28 * NA_)}', ['C1: thickness = V/A', 'C1: m = ρV', 'C1: N = (m/M)N_A'], aq='N')
M('M', 3, 'Two flasks of equal volume at the same temperature and pressure contain hydrogen and carbon dioxide. Which is correct?', 'They contain the same number of molecules but the CO₂ has 22 times the mass', ['They contain the same mass of gas', 'The hydrogen flask has 22 times as many molecules', 'The CO₂ molecules move faster'], 'pV = NkT: same p, V, T means same N. Molar masses 2 and 44 g mol⁻¹ give a mass ratio of 22.', ['C1: same N from pV = NkT', 'C1: mass ratio 44/2'])
N('M', 3, 'A sample of 1.2 × 10²³ molecules has a mass of 8.0 g. Calculate the mass of one molecule, in units of 10⁻²⁶ kg.', 0.008 / 1.2e23, '10⁻²⁶ kg', f'm = 8.0 × 10⁻³ ÷ 1.2 × 10²³ = {sf(0.008 / 1.2e23)} kg', ['C1: mass in kg', 'C1: ÷ number'], aq='m')
M('M', 4, 'One mole of water is about 18 cm³ of liquid but about 22 400 cm³ as steam at 273 K and 1 atm (if it were an ideal gas). What does this show?', 'The molecules in the gas are on average about 11 times further apart than in the liquid', ['The molecules in steam are larger', 'Steam contains more molecules per mole', 'The molecules in the liquid are about 1000 times further apart'], 'Volume ratio ≈ 1240, so the spacing ratio ≈ ∛1240 ≈ 11.', ['C1: same number of molecules', 'C1: volume ratio', 'C1: cube root'])
N('M', 4, 'A diamond (pure carbon, molar mass 0.012 kg mol⁻¹) has mass 0.20 g (a one-carat stone is 0.20 g). Calculate the number of carbon atoms in the stone.' + take('NA'), 0.0002 / 0.012 * NA_, None, f'n = 2.0 × 10⁻⁴ ÷ 0.012 = {sf(2e-4 / 0.012)} mol. N = {sf(2e-4 / 0.012, 4)} × 6.02 × 10²³ = {sf(2e-4 / 0.012 * NA_)}', ['C1: mass in kg', 'C1: n = m/M', 'C1: N = nN_A'], aq='N')
N('M', 3, 'How many moles of air are in a classroom 8.0 m × 7.0 m × 3.0 m, if air has density 1.2 kg m⁻³ and molar mass 0.029 kg mol⁻¹?', 8 * 7 * 3 * 1.2 / 0.029, 'mol', f'V = 168 m³, m = 1.2 × 168 = 201.6 kg, n = 201.6 ÷ 0.029 = {sf(201.6 / 0.029)} mol', ['C1: volume', 'C1: m = ρV', 'C1: n = m/M'], aq='n')
# hard
N('H', 6, 'A sample of an unknown gas has mass 2.4 g. At 300 K and 1.0 × 10⁵ Pa it occupies 1.85 × 10⁻³ m³. (a) Calculate the number of moles, using pV = nRT. (b) Calculate the molar mass. (c) Suggest the identity of the gas, and calculate the number of molecules. Give only the answer to (b).' + take('R', 'NA'), 0.0024 / (1.0e5 * 1.85e-3 / (8.31 * 300)), 'kg mol⁻¹',
  f'(a) n = pV/RT = 1.0 × 10⁵ × 1.85 × 10⁻³ ÷ (8.31 × 300) = {sf(1e5 * 1.85e-3 / 2493)} mol. (b) M = m/n = 0.0024 ÷ {sf(1e5 * 1.85e-3 / 2493, 4)} = {sf(0.0024 / (1e5 * 1.85e-3 / 2493))} kg mol⁻¹. (c) About 32 g mol⁻¹: oxygen. N = {sf(1e5 * 1.85e-3 / 2493, 4)} × 6.02 × 10²³ = {sf(1e5 * 1.85e-3 / 2493 * NA_)}',
  ['C1: n = pV/RT', 'A1: n', 'C1: M = m/n', 'A1: M', 'B1: oxygen', 'B1: N'], aq='M')
N('H', 5, 'A human body contains about 45 kg of water (molar mass 0.018 kg mol⁻¹). (a) Calculate the number of moles of water. (b) Calculate the number of water molecules. (c) Each molecule has 2 hydrogen atoms. Calculate the number of hydrogen atoms. Give only the answer to (c).' + take('NA'), 2 * 45 / 0.018 * NA_, None,
  f'(a) n = 45 ÷ 0.018 = 2500 mol. (b) N = 2500 × 6.02 × 10²³ = {sf(2500 * NA_)}. (c) 2 × {sf(2500 * NA_, 4)} = {sf(5000 * NA_)}',
  ['C1: n = m/M', 'A1: 2500 mol', 'C1: N = nN_A', 'A1: N', 'A1: × 2'], aq='N_H')
N('H', 6, 'In an oil-drop experiment, an oil drop of volume 1.2 × 10⁻⁹ m³ spreads on water into a circular film of diameter 0.25 m. The oil has density 870 kg m⁻³ and molar mass 0.26 kg mol⁻¹. (a) Estimate the thickness of the film. (b) Assume the film is one molecule thick and each molecule is a cube. Estimate the volume of one molecule. (c) Estimate the Avogadro constant. Give only the answer to (c).',
  0.26 / (870 * (1.2e-9 / (pi * 0.125 ** 2)) ** 3), 'mol⁻¹',
  f'(a) t = V/A = 1.2 × 10⁻⁹ ÷ (π × 0.125²) = {sf(1.2e-9 / (pi * 0.125 ** 2))} m. (b) V₁ = t³ = ({sf(1.2e-9 / (pi * 0.125 ** 2), 4)})³ = {sf((1.2e-9 / (pi * 0.125 ** 2)) ** 3)} m³. (c) Volume of one mole = M/ρ = 0.26 ÷ 870 = {sf(0.26 / 870)} m³, so N_A ≈ {sf(0.26 / 870, 4)} ÷ {sf((1.2e-9 / (pi * 0.125 ** 2)) ** 3, 4)} = {sf(0.26 / (870 * (1.2e-9 / (pi * 0.125 ** 2)) ** 3))} mol⁻¹ (the right order of magnitude).',
  ['C1: t = V/A', 'A1: t', 'C1: V₁ = t³', 'C1: molar volume M/ρ', 'C1: N_A = (M/ρ)/V₁', 'A1: N_A'], aq='N_A')
N('H', 5, 'A 1.0 m³ container holds a mixture of 2.0 mol of nitrogen (M = 0.028 kg mol⁻¹) and 0.50 mol of oxygen (M = 0.032 kg mol⁻¹). (a) Calculate the total number of molecules. (b) Calculate the total mass. (c) Calculate the mean molar mass of the mixture. Give only the answer to (c).' + take('NA'), (2.0 * 0.028 + 0.5 * 0.032) / 2.5, 'kg mol⁻¹',
  f'(a) N = 2.5 × 6.02 × 10²³ = {sf(2.5 * NA_)}. (b) m = 2.0 × 0.028 + 0.50 × 0.032 = 0.072 kg. (c) M = m/n = 0.072 ÷ 2.5 = {sf(0.072 / 2.5)} kg mol⁻¹',
  ['A1: N', 'C1: masses added', 'A1: m', 'C1: M = m/n', 'A1: M'], aq='M')
N('H', 6, 'Silicon has density 2330 kg m⁻³ and molar mass 0.0281 kg mol⁻¹. Its atoms are arranged so that each occupies a volume of 2.0 × 10⁻²⁹ m³. (a) Calculate the number of atoms in 1.0 m³. (b) Calculate the mass of one atom. (c) Hence calculate a value of the Avogadro constant from these data. Give only the answer to (c).', 0.0281 / (2330 * 2.0e-29), 'mol⁻¹',
  f'(a) 1.0 ÷ 2.0 × 10⁻²⁹ = 5.0 × 10²⁸ atoms. (b) m = 2330 ÷ 5.0 × 10²⁸ = {sf(2330 / 5e28)} kg. (c) N_A = M/m = 0.0281 ÷ {sf(2330 / 5e28, 4)} = {sf(0.0281 / (2330 / 5e28))} mol⁻¹',
  ['C1: atoms per m³', 'A1: 5.0 × 10²⁸', 'C1: mass of one atom', 'A1: m', 'C1: N_A = M/m', 'A1: N_A'], aq='N_A')
N('H', 5, 'A reaction produces 0.0050 mol of hydrogen gas (H₂) every second. (a) Calculate the number of molecules produced per second. (b) Calculate the mass produced per minute (M = 0.0020 kg mol⁻¹). (c) Calculate the time to produce 1.0 × 10²⁴ molecules. Give only the answer to (c).' + take('NA'), 1.0e24 / (0.0050 * NA_), 's',
  f'(a) 0.0050 × 6.02 × 10²³ = {sf(0.005 * NA_)} s⁻¹. (b) 0.0050 × 60 × 0.0020 = 6.0 × 10⁻⁴ kg. (c) t = 1.0 × 10²⁴ ÷ {sf(0.005 * NA_, 4)} = {sf(1e24 / (0.005 * NA_))} s',
  ['A1: rate', 'C1: mass per minute', 'A1: 6.0 × 10⁻⁴ kg', 'C1: t = N/rate', 'A1: t'], aq='t')

# ═════════════════════════ 20.4 Boyle's law (17/20/13) ═════════════════════════
lesson('20.4')
M('E', 1, 'Boyle\'s law states that for a fixed mass of gas at constant temperature…', 'the pressure is inversely proportional to the volume', ['the pressure is proportional to the volume', 'the volume is proportional to the temperature', 'the pressure is proportional to the temperature'], 'pV = constant when n and T are fixed.')
N('E', 2, 'A gas occupies 0.60 m³ at 1.0 × 10⁵ Pa. It is compressed at constant temperature to 0.20 m³. Calculate the new pressure.', 1.0e5 * 0.60 / 0.20, 'Pa', 'p₂ = p₁V₁/V₂ = 1.0 × 10⁵ × 0.60 ÷ 0.20 = 3.0 × 10⁵ Pa', ['C1: p₁V₁ = p₂V₂'], sig=2, aq='p')
N('E', 2, 'A syringe holds 40 cm³ of air at 100 kPa. The plunger is pushed in slowly until the pressure is 160 kPa. Calculate the new volume.', 40 * 100 / 160, 'cm³', 'V₂ = p₁V₁/p₂ = 100 × 40 ÷ 160 = 25 cm³', ['C1: p₁V₁ = p₂V₂'], sig=2, aq='V')
M('E', 1, 'Which quantities must be kept constant for Boyle\'s law to apply?', 'The mass of gas and its temperature', ['The pressure and temperature', 'The volume and temperature', 'The mass and the pressure'], 'Boyle\'s law: fixed amount of gas at constant temperature.')
M('E', 1, 'A graph of p against 1/V for a gas obeying Boyle\'s law is…', 'a straight line through the origin', ['a curve getting steeper', 'a horizontal line', 'a straight line with negative gradient'], 'p = constant × (1/V): proportional, so a line through the origin.')
N('E', 2, 'A bubble of air has a volume of 2.0 cm³ at the bottom of a lake, where the pressure is 3.0 × 10⁵ Pa. What is its volume just below the surface, where the pressure is 1.0 × 10⁵ Pa? (Assume constant temperature.)', 2.0 * 3.0, 'cm³', 'V₂ = p₁V₁/p₂ = 3.0 × 10⁵ × 2.0 ÷ 1.0 × 10⁵ = 6.0 cm³', ['C1: p₁V₁ = p₂V₂'], sig=2, aq='V')
M('E', 1, 'A graph of p against V for a gas at constant temperature is…', 'a curve (hyperbola) with p decreasing as V increases', ['a straight line through the origin', 'a straight line rising', 'a horizontal line'], 'p ∝ 1/V gives a hyperbola, called an isothermal.')
N('E', 2, 'A diver\'s lungs hold 5.5 litres of air at 2.0 × 10⁵ Pa at depth. If the diver rose without exhaling, what volume would the air expand to at 1.0 × 10⁵ Pa (same temperature)?', 5.5 * 2.0, 'litres', 'V₂ = p₁V₁/p₂ = 2.0 × 10⁵ × 5.5 ÷ 1.0 × 10⁵ = 11 litres — which is why divers must breathe out as they rise', ['C1: p₁V₁ = p₂V₂'], sig=2, aq='V')
M('E', 1, 'In a Boyle\'s law experiment, why is the pump moved slowly?', 'So the gas stays at the same temperature (heat can flow in or out)', ['To keep the mass of gas constant', 'To stop the gauge breaking', 'To keep the volume constant'], 'Fast compression heats the gas; slow changes keep it isothermal.')
N('E', 2, 'A gas has pV = 2400 J at a certain temperature. What is its pressure when its volume is 0.030 m³?', 2400 / 0.030, 'Pa', 'p = 2400 ÷ 0.030 = 8.0 × 10⁴ Pa', ['C1: p = constant/V'], sig=2, aq='p')
M('E', 1, 'The volume of a fixed mass of gas at constant temperature is doubled. What happens to its pressure?', 'It halves', ['It doubles', 'It quarters', 'It stays the same'], 'At constant temperature pV is constant, so p ∝ 1/V: doubling V halves p.')
N('E', 2, 'A tyre pump cylinder contains 120 cm³ of air at 1.0 × 10⁵ Pa. Calculate the pressure when the air is squeezed into 40 cm³ at constant temperature.', 1.0e5 * 120 / 40, 'Pa', 'p₂ = 1.0 × 10⁵ × 120 ÷ 40 = 3.0 × 10⁵ Pa', ['C1: p₁V₁ = p₂V₂'], sig=2, aq='p')
M('E', 1, 'Which units can be used for V in p₁V₁ = p₂V₂?', 'Any volume unit, as long as both volumes use the same unit', ['Only m³', 'Only cm³', 'Only litres'], 'The units cancel in the ratio V₁/V₂.')
N('E', 2, 'A sealed syringe of air at 101 kPa is pulled out from 15 cm³ to 45 cm³ at constant temperature. Calculate the new pressure in kPa.', 101 * 15 / 45, 'kPa', 'p₂ = 101 × 15 ÷ 45 = 33.7 kPa', ['C1: p₁V₁ = p₂V₂'], aq='p')
M('E', 1, 'For a gas obeying Boyle\'s law, what is constant?', 'The product of pressure and volume', ['The ratio of pressure to volume', 'The sum of pressure and volume', 'The pressure only'], 'pV = constant at fixed n and T.')
N('E', 2, 'An aerosol can holds 0.40 litres of gas at 4.0 × 10⁵ Pa. What volume would this gas occupy at 1.0 × 10⁵ Pa at the same temperature?', 0.40 * 4.0, 'litres', 'V₂ = 4.0 × 10⁵ × 0.40 ÷ 1.0 × 10⁵ = 1.6 litres', ['C1: p₁V₁ = p₂V₂'], sig=2, aq='V')
M('E', 1, 'Using the kinetic model, why does the pressure of a gas increase when it is compressed at constant temperature?', 'The molecules hit each unit area of the walls more often', ['The molecules move faster', 'The molecules get heavier', 'More molecules are created'], 'Same speeds, higher number density, so more collisions per second per unit area.')
N('E', 2, 'A weather balloon holds 12 m³ of helium at 100 kPa on the ground. At high altitude the pressure is 30 kPa. Assuming the temperature is unchanged, calculate its volume there.', 12 * 100 / 30, 'm³', 'V₂ = 100 × 12 ÷ 30 = 40 m³', ['C1: p₁V₁ = p₂V₂'], sig=2, aq='V')
# medium
N('M', 3, 'A gas at 1.2 × 10⁵ Pa occupies 3.0 litres. Calculate the change in volume when the pressure is increased to 1.8 × 10⁵ Pa at constant temperature.', 3.0 - 3.0 * 1.2 / 1.8, 'litres', 'V₂ = 3.0 × 1.2 ÷ 1.8 = 2.0 litres, so the volume decreases by 3.0 − 2.0 = 1.0 litre', ['C1: p₁V₁ = p₂V₂', 'C1: V₂', 'C1: change'], sig=2, aq='ΔV')
N('M', 4, 'A bubble rises from the bottom of a 15 m deep lake to the surface. At the surface the pressure is 1.0 × 10⁵ Pa; at the bottom it is 1.0 × 10⁵ + ρgh. The bubble\'s volume at the bottom is 1.5 cm³. Calculate its volume at the surface (ρ = 1000 kg m⁻³, constant temperature).' + take('g'), 1.5 * (1.0e5 + 1000 * 9.81 * 15) / 1.0e5, 'cm³',
  f'p_bottom = 1.0 × 10⁵ + 1000 × 9.81 × 15 = {sf(1e5 + 147150)} Pa. V = 1.5 × {sf(1e5 + 147150, 4)} ÷ 1.0 × 10⁵ = {sf(1.5 * 2.4715)} cm³', ['C1: p = p₀ + ρgh', 'C1: p₁V₁ = p₂V₂', 'C1: substitution'], aq='V')
M('M', 3, 'A student plots p against 1/V for air in a syringe and gets a straight line that does not quite pass through the origin. Which is the most likely reason?', 'The air in the connecting tube was not included in V', ['The temperature was constant', 'Air does not obey Boyle\'s law', 'The pressure gauge read zero at atmospheric pressure'], 'A systematic error in V (a small unmeasured extra volume) shifts the line.', ['C1: systematic error', 'C1: unmeasured volume'])
N('M', 3, 'A cylinder of compressed air has volume 12 litres at 2.0 × 10⁷ Pa. How many 2.0-litre balloons can it fill at 1.0 × 10⁵ Pa, at constant temperature? (The cylinder keeps 12 litres at 1.0 × 10⁵ Pa when it is "empty".)', (12 * 2.0e7 / 1.0e5 - 12) / 2.0, None, 'Total volume at 1.0 × 10⁵ Pa = 12 × 2.0 × 10⁷ ÷ 1.0 × 10⁵ = 2400 litres. Usable = 2400 − 12 = 2388 litres. Balloons = 2388 ÷ 2.0 = 1194', ['C1: total volume at atmospheric pressure', 'C1: subtract residual 12 litres', 'C1: ÷ 2.0'], aq='number', tol=0.01)
M('M', 3, 'On a p–V graph, two isothermals (Boyle\'s-law curves) are drawn for the same gas. Which is at the higher temperature?', 'The one further from the origin', ['The one closer to the origin', 'They are at the same temperature', 'The steeper one at small V'], 'pV = nRT: a higher T gives a larger pV, so the curve is further out.', ['C1: pV ∝ T', 'C1: curve further out'])
N('M', 4, 'A gas is held in a 25 cm long tube by a column of mercury. At atmospheric pressure (76 cm Hg) the trapped air column is 20 cm long. The tube is turned so the extra mercury pressure on the air is 15 cm Hg. Calculate the new length of the air column (constant temperature, uniform tube).', 20 * 76 / 91, 'cm', 'p₁ = 76 cm Hg, p₂ = 76 + 15 = 91 cm Hg. L₂ = L₁p₁/p₂ = 20 × 76 ÷ 91 = 16.7 cm', ['C1: total pressure', 'C1: V ∝ L for uniform tube', 'C1: p₁L₁ = p₂L₂'], aq='L')
N('M', 3, 'In a Boyle\'s-law experiment, pV for air is found to be 101 J at p = 1.01 × 10⁵ Pa. Calculate the volume when p = 2.50 × 10⁵ Pa.', 101 / 2.50e5, 'm³', 'V = 101 ÷ 2.50 × 10⁵ = 4.04 × 10⁻⁴ m³', ['C1: pV = constant', 'C1: V = constant/p'], aq='V')
M('M', 4, 'A gas is compressed rapidly from 1.0 litre to 0.50 litre and its pressure is measured immediately. The pressure is more than double the starting pressure. Why?', 'The fast compression heated the gas, so Boyle\'s law (constant temperature) did not apply', ['Some gas leaked in', 'Boyle\'s law only applies to liquids', 'The volume was measured wrongly'], 'Work done on the gas raised its temperature; pV increased.', ['C1: rapid compression heats', 'C1: not isothermal', 'C1: pV increases'])
N('M', 3, 'A balloon has volume 4.0 litres at sea level (101 kPa). At the top of a mountain it has expanded to 5.5 litres at the same temperature. Calculate the air pressure at the top.', 101 * 4.0 / 5.5, 'kPa', 'p₂ = 101 × 4.0 ÷ 5.5 = 73.5 kPa', ['C1: p₁V₁ = p₂V₂', 'C1: substitution'], aq='p')
N('M', 3, 'A gas in a flexible container obeys Boyle\'s law. When the pressure rises by 25 %, by what percentage does the volume fall?', (1 - 1 / 1.25) * 100, '%', 'V₂/V₁ = 1/1.25 = 0.80, so the volume falls by (1 − 0.80) × 100 = 20 %', ['C1: V ∝ 1/p', 'C1: percentage'], sig=2, aq='decrease')
M('M', 3, 'Why does Boyle\'s law fail for real gases at very high pressure?', 'The molecules\' own volume and the attractive forces between them become significant', ['The temperature always rises', 'The molecules stop moving', 'The pressure cannot be measured'], 'At high density the ideal-gas assumptions no longer hold.', ['C1: molecular volume', 'C1: intermolecular forces'])
N('M', 4, 'A bicycle pump has a 30 cm long barrel. The outlet valve opens when the pressure inside reaches 4.0 × 10⁵ Pa. Starting with air at 1.0 × 10⁵ Pa in the full barrel, how far must the piston be pushed before air enters the tyre? (Assume constant temperature.)', 30 - 30 * 1.0 / 4.0, 'cm', 'L₂ = 30 × 1.0 × 10⁵ ÷ 4.0 × 10⁵ = 7.5 cm, so the piston moves 30 − 7.5 = 22.5 cm', ['C1: p₁L₁ = p₂L₂', 'C1: L₂', 'C1: distance moved'], aq='d')
N('M', 3, 'A sealed bag of crisps has 300 cm³ of air at 101 kPa. It is taken on a plane where the cabin pressure is 75 kPa. Calculate the new volume of air, at constant temperature.', 300 * 101 / 75, 'cm³', 'V₂ = 300 × 101 ÷ 75 = 404 cm³ — the bag puffs up', ['C1: p₁V₁ = p₂V₂', 'C1: substitution'], aq='V')
M('M', 3, 'A gas obeys Boyle\'s law. Its pressure is plotted against volume and also against 1/V. Which statement is correct?', 'The p–1/V graph is linear through the origin, which is easier to test than the p–V curve', ['The p–V graph is linear', 'Both graphs are curves', 'The p–1/V graph has a negative gradient'], 'p = k(1/V): a straight line through the origin with gradient k = pV.', ['C1: p ∝ 1/V', 'C1: straight line test'])
N('M', 4, 'Two flasks are joined by a closed tap. Flask A (2.0 litres) holds gas at 3.0 × 10⁵ Pa; flask B (3.0 litres) is evacuated. The tap is opened. Calculate the final pressure, at constant temperature.', 3.0e5 * 2.0 / 5.0, 'Pa', 'p₂ = p₁V₁/V₂ = 3.0 × 10⁵ × 2.0 ÷ (2.0 + 3.0) = 1.2 × 10⁵ Pa', ['C1: total volume 5.0 litres', 'C1: p₁V₁ = p₂V₂', 'C1: substitution'], sig=2, aq='p')
N('M', 3, 'At constant temperature, pV for a sample of gas is 3600 J. Calculate the pressure when the volume is 4.5 litres.', 3600 / 4.5e-3, 'Pa', 'V = 4.5 × 10⁻³ m³. p = 3600 ÷ 4.5 × 10⁻³ = 8.0 × 10⁵ Pa', ['C1: litres to m³', 'C1: p = constant/V'], sig=2, aq='p')
M('M', 3, 'A syringe of gas is compressed to a quarter of its volume at constant temperature. What happens to the number of molecules per unit volume and to the mean kinetic energy?', 'Number per unit volume ×4; mean kinetic energy unchanged', ['Both ×4', 'Number unchanged; mean kinetic energy ×4', 'Both unchanged'], 'Same molecules in ¼ the volume, same temperature.', ['C1: density ×4', 'C1: T unchanged'])
N('M', 4, 'A gas cylinder at 1.5 × 10⁷ Pa supplies gas to a welding torch at 2.0 × 10⁵ Pa. The cylinder volume is 0.050 m³. Calculate the volume of gas at the torch pressure that can be supplied before the cylinder pressure falls to 2.0 × 10⁵ Pa (constant temperature).', 0.050 * 1.5e7 / 2.0e5 - 0.050, 'm³', 'At 2.0 × 10⁵ Pa the gas would occupy 0.050 × 1.5 × 10⁷ ÷ 2.0 × 10⁵ = 3.75 m³. The cylinder keeps 0.050 m³, so 3.75 − 0.050 = 3.70 m³ is supplied', ['C1: p₁V₁ = p₂V₂', 'C1: subtract cylinder volume', 'C1: substitution'], aq='V')
N('M', 3, 'A student records: V = 20 cm³, p = 150 kPa; V = 30 cm³, p = 100 kPa; V = 40 cm³, p = 75 kPa. Show that the data obey Boyle\'s law and find the pressure at V = 60 cm³.', 3000 / 60, 'kPa', 'pV = 20 × 150 = 30 × 100 = 40 × 75 = 3000 kPa cm³: constant. At 60 cm³, p = 3000 ÷ 60 = 50 kPa', ['C1: pV for each', 'B1: constant', 'C1: p = 3000/60'], sig=2, aq='p')
# hard
N('H', 5, 'A diver at a depth where the pressure is 3.5 × 10⁵ Pa breathes out a bubble of volume 3.0 cm³. The water temperature is constant. (a) Calculate the bubble\'s volume at the surface (1.0 × 10⁵ Pa). (b) Calculate its volume halfway up, where the pressure is 2.25 × 10⁵ Pa. (c) Explain, using the kinetic model, why the bubble grows. Give only the answer to (a).', 3.0 * 3.5, 'cm³',
  '(a) V = 3.5 × 10⁵ × 3.0 ÷ 1.0 × 10⁵ = 10.5 cm³. (b) V = 3.5 × 10⁵ × 3.0 ÷ 2.25 × 10⁵ = 4.67 cm³. (c) The water pressure outside falls; the gas expands until its molecules hit the walls less often, so its pressure matches the lower outside pressure.',
  ['C1: p₁V₁ = p₂V₂', 'A1: 10.5 cm³', 'A1: 4.67 cm³', 'B1: lower outside pressure', 'B1: fewer collisions per area'], aq='V')
N('H', 6, 'A student tests Boyle\'s law with a syringe connected to a pressure gauge by a tube of internal volume 2.0 cm³ (not included in the syringe reading). Syringe readings: 18.0 cm³ at 100 kPa and 8.0 cm³ at p₂. (a) Calculate p₂ if the tube volume is ignored. (b) Calculate p₂ allowing for the tube. (c) Explain which is correct and the effect on a p–1/V graph. Give only the answer to (b).', 100 * 20.0 / 10.0, 'kPa',
  '(a) p₂ = 100 × 18.0 ÷ 8.0 = 225 kPa. (b) Total volumes 20.0 and 10.0 cm³: p₂ = 100 × 20.0 ÷ 10.0 = 200 kPa. (c) (b) is correct; ignoring the tube gives a straight p–1/V line that does not pass through the origin.',
  ['C1: ignoring tube', 'A1: 225 kPa', 'C1: total volumes', 'A1: 200 kPa', 'B1: (b) correct', 'B1: line misses origin'], sig=3, aq='p')
N('H', 5, 'A sealed glass bulb of volume 50 cm³ contains air at 1.0 × 10⁵ Pa. It is connected via a tap to an evacuated bulb. When the tap is opened the pressure falls to 3.8 × 10⁴ Pa at the same temperature. (a) Calculate the total volume. (b) Calculate the volume of the second bulb. (c) Explain why the temperature might drop slightly at first. Give only the answer to (b).', 50 * 1.0e5 / 3.8e4 - 50, 'cm³',
  f'(a) V = 50 × 1.0 × 10⁵ ÷ 3.8 × 10⁴ = {sf(50 / 0.38)} cm³. (b) V₂ = {sf(50 / 0.38, 4)} − 50 = {sf(50 / 0.38 - 50)} cm³. (c) If the expansion is fast, the gas does work pushing gas into the other bulb and briefly cools before reaching equilibrium with the glass.',
  ['C1: p₁V₁ = p₂V₂', 'A1: total V', 'C1: subtract', 'A1: V₂', 'B1: rapid expansion cools'], aq='V')
N('H', 6, 'Data for air at constant temperature: V/cm³ = 10, 15, 20, 25, 30; p/kPa = 300, 200, 150, 120, 100. (a) Calculate pV for each reading and comment. (b) Describe the graph of p against 1/V, giving its gradient with units. (c) Calculate the number of moles of air at 293 K. Give only the answer to (c).' + take('R'), 3000e3 * 1e-6 / (8.31 * 293), 'mol',
  '(a) pV = 3000 kPa cm³ for every reading: Boyle\'s law is obeyed. (b) A straight line through the origin with gradient pV = 3000 kPa cm³ = 3.0 J. (c) n = pV/RT = 3.0 ÷ (8.31 × 293) = 1.23 × 10⁻³ mol',
  ['M1: pV for each', 'A1: constant 3000 kPa cm³', 'B1: straight line through origin', 'C1: gradient = pV = 3.0 J', 'C1: n = pV/RT', 'A1: n'], aq='n')
N('H', 5, 'A car tyre contains 0.012 m³ of air at 3.2 × 10⁵ Pa. A puncture lets air leak out slowly at constant temperature until the pressure is 1.0 × 10⁵ Pa (atmospheric). (a) Calculate the volume the original air would occupy at 1.0 × 10⁵ Pa. (b) Calculate the volume of air (at atmospheric pressure) that leaked out. (c) Calculate the fraction of the original air that escaped. Give only the answer to (c).', (0.012 * 3.2 - 0.012) / (0.012 * 3.2), None,
  '(a) V = 0.012 × 3.2 × 10⁵ ÷ 1.0 × 10⁵ = 0.0384 m³. (b) The tyre keeps 0.012 m³, so 0.0384 − 0.012 = 0.0264 m³ leaked. (c) fraction = 0.0264 ÷ 0.0384 = 0.6875',
  ['C1: Boyle\'s law', 'A1: 0.0384 m³', 'A1: 0.0264 m³', 'C1: fraction', 'A1: 0.69'], aq='fraction')
N('H', 6, 'A vertical cylinder holds air below a frictionless piston of mass 10 kg and area 5.0 × 10⁻³ m². Atmospheric pressure is 1.0 × 10⁵ Pa. The air column is 20 cm high. A 5.0 kg mass is placed on the piston, at constant temperature. (a) Calculate the initial pressure of the trapped air. (b) Calculate the new pressure. (c) Calculate the new height of the air column. Give only the answer to (c).' + take('g'), 20 * (1.0e5 + 10 * 9.81 / 5.0e-3) / (1.0e5 + 15 * 9.81 / 5.0e-3), 'cm',
  f'(a) p₁ = 1.0 × 10⁵ + 10 × 9.81 ÷ 5.0 × 10⁻³ = {sf(1e5 + 98.1 / 5e-3)} Pa. (b) p₂ = 1.0 × 10⁵ + 15 × 9.81 ÷ 5.0 × 10⁻³ = {sf(1e5 + 147.15 / 5e-3)} Pa. (c) h₂ = h₁p₁/p₂ = 20 × {sf(1e5 + 98.1 / 5e-3, 4)} ÷ {sf(1e5 + 147.15 / 5e-3, 4)} = {sf(20 * (1e5 + 98.1 / 5e-3) / (1e5 + 147.15 / 5e-3))} cm',
  ['C1: p = p₀ + mg/A', 'A1: p₁', 'A1: p₂', 'M1: p₁h₁ = p₂h₂', 'C1: substitution', 'A1: h₂'], aq='h')
N('H', 5, 'Explain why a weather balloon, filled with helium at ground level, bursts at high altitude. A balloon holds 6.0 m³ at 100 kPa and 290 K; it bursts at 18 m³. Assuming the temperature stays at 290 K: (a) calculate the pressure at bursting; (b) the air temperature at altitude is really 220 K. Use pV/T = constant to calculate the pressure at bursting at 220 K. Give only the answer to (b).', 100 * 6.0 / 290 * 220 / 18, 'kPa',
  f'(a) p = 100 × 6.0 ÷ 18 = 33.3 kPa. (b) p₂ = p₁V₁T₂/(T₁V₂) = 100 × 6.0 × 220 ÷ (290 × 18) = {sf(100 * 6 * 220 / (290 * 18))} kPa — the balloon must rise even higher, to a lower pressure, before it bursts. As the outside pressure falls the helium expands until the rubber can stretch no further.',
  ['C1: Boyle\'s law', 'A1: 33.3 kPa', 'C1: pV/T constant', 'A1: p₂', 'B1: expansion until burst'], aq='p')
N('H', 6, 'A cylinder of gas has a pressure gauge reading 2.0 × 10⁶ Pa (absolute). It supplies 1.5 litres per minute of gas at 1.0 × 10⁵ Pa to a patient. The cylinder volume is 10 litres and the gas temperature is constant. (a) Calculate the volume of gas available at 1.0 × 10⁵ Pa, leaving the cylinder at 1.0 × 10⁵ Pa. (b) Calculate how long the cylinder lasts. (c) Calculate the cylinder pressure after 1.0 hour. Give only the answer to (b), in minutes.', (10 * 2.0e6 / 1.0e5 - 10) / 1.5, 'minutes',
  '(a) V = 10 × 2.0 × 10⁶ ÷ 1.0 × 10⁵ − 10 = 200 − 10 = 190 litres. (b) t = 190 ÷ 1.5 = 127 minutes. (c) After 60 min, 90 litres used: 200 − 90 = 110 litres-at-1 atm remain in 10 litres, so p = 1.0 × 10⁵ × 110 ÷ 10 = 1.1 × 10⁶ Pa.',
  ['C1: Boyle\'s law', 'C1: subtract residual', 'A1: 190 litres', 'C1: t = V/rate', 'A1: t', 'B1: pressure after 1 h'], aq='t')
N('H', 5, 'A U-tube manometer with water is used to measure the pressure of a gas being compressed in a syringe. Initially the levels are equal (gas at 101 kPa) and the gas volume is 50 cm³. After compression the water levels differ by 34 cm. (a) Calculate the pressure difference, ρgh. (b) Calculate the new gas pressure. (c) Calculate the new volume at constant temperature. Give only the answer to (c).' + take('g'), 50 * 101e3 / (101e3 + 1000 * 9.81 * 0.34), 'cm³',
  f'(a) Δp = 1000 × 9.81 × 0.34 = {sf(1000 * 9.81 * 0.34)} Pa. (b) p = 101000 + {sf(3335.4, 4)} = {sf(101000 + 3335.4)} Pa. (c) V = 50 × 101000 ÷ {sf(104335.4, 6)} = {sf(50 * 101e3 / 104335.4)} cm³',
  ['C1: Δp = ρgh', 'A1: Δp', 'A1: p', 'C1: Boyle\'s law', 'A1: V'], aq='V')
N('H', 6, 'An explorer\'s sealed food container holds 1.5 litres of air at 1.01 × 10⁵ Pa at sea level. (a) At the top of a mountain the air pressure is 6.0 × 10⁴ Pa. Calculate the resultant force on the 0.020 m² lid due to the pressure difference, assuming the volume stays fixed. (b) If the lid were free to rise, calculate the volume the air would expand to (constant temperature). Give only the answer to (a).', (1.01e5 - 6.0e4) * 0.020, 'N',
  '(a) Δp = 1.01 × 10⁵ − 6.0 × 10⁴ = 4.1 × 10⁴ Pa, F = ΔpA = 4.1 × 10⁴ × 0.020 = 820 N (pushing the lid outwards). (b) V = 1.5 × 1.01 × 10⁵ ÷ 6.0 × 10⁴ = 2.5 litres.',
  ['C1: pressure difference', 'C1: F = ΔpA', 'A1: F', 'B1: direction', 'C1: Boyle\'s law', 'A1: V'], aq='F')

# ── extra questions to reach 50 (20.1) ──
lesson('20.1')
N('M', 3, 'A person breathes in 0.50 litres of air. Under room conditions one mole of any gas occupies about 0.024 m³. Estimate the number of molecules taken in with each breath.' + take('NA'), 0.50e-3 / 0.024 * NA_, None,
  f'V = 0.50 litres = 5.0 × 10⁻⁴ m³. n = 5.0 × 10⁻⁴ ÷ 0.024 = {sf(5e-4 / 0.024)} mol. N = {sf(5e-4 / 0.024, 4)} × 6.02 × 10²³ = {sf(5e-4 / 0.024 * NA_)}',
  ['C1: litres converted to m³', 'C1: n = V/V_m'], aq='N')
N('H', 5, 'Liquid water has density 1000 kg m⁻³. At 100 °C and atmospheric pressure the steam above it has density 0.60 kg m⁻³. The molar mass of water is 0.018 kg mol⁻¹. (a) Calculate the volume available to each molecule in the liquid. (b) Calculate the volume available to each molecule in the steam. (c) Hence calculate the ratio of the mean spacing of molecules in the steam to that in the liquid, and comment on what this means for the kinetic-theory assumptions. Give only the ratio from (c).' + take('NA'), (1000 / 0.60) ** (1 / 3), None,
  f'(a) V₁ = 0.018 ÷ (1000 × 6.02 × 10²³) = {sf(0.018 / (1000 * NA_))} m³. (b) V₂ = 0.018 ÷ (0.60 × 6.02 × 10²³) = {sf(0.018 / (0.6 * NA_))} m³. (c) Spacing scales as the cube root of the volume per molecule, so the ratio is (1000 ÷ 0.60)^(1/3) = {sf((1000 / 0.6) ** (1 / 3))}. In the vapour the molecules are about twelve diameters apart, so their own volume and the forces between them are small, as the ideal-gas model assumes.',
  ['C1: volume per molecule in liquid = M/(ρN_A)', 'C1: volume per molecule in steam', 'M1: spacing ∝ (volume per molecule)^(1/3)', 'B1: molecules far apart, so ideal-gas assumptions reasonable'], aq='ratio')
N('H', 5, 'Air under room conditions contains about 2.5 × 10²⁵ molecules per cubic metre. (a) Calculate the number of molecules in 1.0 cm³ of air. (b) A machine could count 1.0 × 10⁹ molecules every second. Calculate how long it would take to count the molecules in 1.0 cm³, in years (1 year = 3.15 × 10⁷ s). (c) Comment on what this suggests about the size of molecules. Give only the answer to (b), in years.', 2.5e25 * 1e-6 / 1e9 / 3.15e7, 'years',
  f'(a) 1.0 cm³ = 1.0 × 10⁻⁶ m³, so N = 2.5 × 10²⁵ × 1.0 × 10⁻⁶ = 2.5 × 10¹⁹. (b) t = 2.5 × 10¹⁹ ÷ 1.0 × 10⁹ = 2.5 × 10¹⁰ s. In years: 2.5 × 10¹⁰ ÷ 3.15 × 10⁷ = {sf(2.5e10 / 3.15e7)}. (c) Even a tiny volume holds an enormous number of molecules, so each molecule must be extremely small.',
  ['C1: 1.0 cm³ = 1.0 × 10⁻⁶ m³', 'C1: N = 2.5 × 10¹⁹', 'C1: t = N ÷ counting rate', 'B1: molecules are extremely small'], aq='t')
N('H', 5, 'A party balloon loses helium through its rubber skin at a steady 2.0 × 10⁻⁴ mol per hour. (a) Calculate the number of helium atoms that escape each second. (b) Calculate the mass of helium lost in 24 hours, in mg (molar mass of helium 4.0 g mol⁻¹). (c) Suggest why helium escapes through the rubber faster than air does. Give only the answer to (a), in s⁻¹.' + take('NA'), 2.0e-4 * NA_ / 3600, 's⁻¹',
  f'(a) Atoms per second = 2.0 × 10⁻⁴ × 6.02 × 10²³ ÷ 3600 = {sf(2e-4 * NA_ / 3600)} s⁻¹. (b) Mass lost = 2.0 × 10⁻⁴ × 24 × 4.0 = 0.0192 g, which is 19.2 mg. (c) Helium atoms are much smaller and lighter than nitrogen or oxygen molecules, so at the same temperature they move faster and slip between the rubber molecules more easily.',
  ['C1: N = nN_A', 'C1: per hour → per second (÷ 3600)', 'C1: mass = n × M over 24 h', 'B1: smaller, faster atoms'], aq='rate')
N('H', 5, 'A gas cylinder holds a mixture of 0.10 kg of helium (molar mass 0.0040 kg mol⁻¹) and 0.10 kg of argon (molar mass 0.040 kg mol⁻¹). (a) Calculate the amount of each gas in moles. (b) Calculate the total number of atoms in the cylinder. (c) Calculate the fraction of the atoms that are helium. (d) Explain why equal masses of the two gases contain such different numbers of atoms. Give only the answer to (c).' + take('NA'), 25 / 27.5, None,
  f'(a) n(He) = 0.10 ÷ 0.0040 = 25 mol. n(Ar) = 0.10 ÷ 0.040 = 2.5 mol. (b) N = 27.5 × 6.02 × 10²³ = {sf(27.5 * NA_)}. (c) Fraction = 25 ÷ 27.5 = {sf(25 / 27.5)}. (d) An argon atom has about ten times the mass of a helium atom, so the same mass contains about one tenth as many atoms.',
  ['C1: n = m/M for both gases', 'C1: total N = (25 + 2.5)N_A', 'M1: fraction = n(He)/total', 'B1: argon atom ten times as massive'], aq='fraction')
N('H', 6, 'In a smoke cell, a smoke particle is a sphere of diameter 1.0 µm and density 1500 kg m⁻³. An air molecule has mass 4.8 × 10⁻²⁶ kg. (a) Calculate the mass of the smoke particle. (b) Calculate how many times more massive the smoke particle is than an air molecule. (c) Explain why the motion of the smoke particle can be seen but the air molecules cannot. (d) The smoke particle and the air molecules have the same mean kinetic energy. Calculate the ratio (r.m.s. speed of air molecules) ÷ (r.m.s. speed of smoke particles). Give only the answer to (d).', sqrt(1500 * pi / 6 * 1e-18 / 4.8e-26), None,
  f'(a) m = 1500 × π/6 × (1.0 × 10⁻⁶)³ = {sf(1500 * pi / 6 * 1e-18)} kg. (b) Mass ratio = {sf(1500 * pi / 6 * 1e-18, 4)} ÷ 4.8 × 10⁻²⁶ = {sf(1500 * pi / 6 * 1e-18 / 4.8e-26)}. (c) The smoke particle is large enough to scatter light into the microscope; the molecules are far too small to see, and only their uneven impacts on the particle reveal them. (d) Equal ½m⟨c²⟩ means c_rms ∝ 1/√m, so the speed ratio = √({sf(1500 * pi / 6 * 1e-18 / 4.8e-26, 4)}) = {sf(sqrt(1500 * pi / 6 * 1e-18 / 4.8e-26))}.',
  ['C1: m = ρ × (π/6)d³', 'C1: mass ratio', 'B1: particle visible, molecules too small', 'M1: equal mean KE so c_rms ∝ 1/√m', 'C1: square root of mass ratio'], aq='ratio')

# ── extra questions to reach 50 (20.2) ──
lesson('20.2')
N('M', 3, 'A sample of methane contains 3.0 × 10²⁵ molecules in every cubic metre. Each molecule has mass 2.66 × 10⁻²⁶ kg and the r.m.s. speed of the molecules is 640 m s⁻¹. Calculate the pressure exerted by the methane.', 3.0e25 * 2.66e-26 * 640 ** 2 / 3, 'Pa',
  f'Density ρ = 3.0 × 10²⁵ × 2.66 × 10⁻²⁶ = {sf(3e25 * 2.66e-26)} kg m⁻³. p = ⅓ρ⟨c²⟩ = {sf(3e25 * 2.66e-26)} × 640² ÷ 3 = {sf(3e25 * 2.66e-26 * 640 ** 2 / 3)} Pa',
  ['C1: ρ = nm (number per m³ × molecular mass)', 'C1: p = ⅓ρ⟨c²⟩ with ⟨c²⟩ = 640²'], aq='p')
M('M', 3, 'A gas molecule of mass m travelling at speed u strikes a flat wall at 60° to the normal and rebounds elastically at 60° on the other side of the normal. What is the magnitude of its change of momentum?', 'mu', ['2mu', 'zero', '√3 mu'],
  'Only the component of velocity perpendicular to the wall, u cos 60° = u/2, reverses. Δp = 2m × u cos 60° = mu. The parallel component u sin 60° is unchanged, so "zero" and "√3 mu" are wrong, and "2mu" applies only to a head-on collision.',
  ['C1: only the normal component reverses', 'C1: Δp = 2mu cos 60°'])
N('H', 5, 'Gas pressure can be modelled with hailstones hitting a roof. During a storm 4.0 × 10⁴ hailstones per second, each of mass 2.0 g, strike a flat horizontal roof of area 50 m². Each arrives vertically at 15 m s⁻¹ and bounces straight back up at 5.0 m s⁻¹. (a) Calculate the change in momentum of one hailstone. (b) Calculate the mean force on the roof. (c) Calculate the mean pressure on the roof due to the hail. (d) State one way in which this model differs from the impacts of gas molecules on a wall. Give only the answer to (c).', 4.0e4 * 0.0020 * 20 / 50, 'Pa',
  f'(a) The velocity reverses, so the speeds add: Δp = 0.0020 × (15 + 5.0) = 0.040 kg m s⁻¹. (b) F = 4.0 × 10⁴ × 0.040 = 1600 N. (c) p = 1600 ÷ 50 = 32 Pa. (d) Gas molecules collide elastically and arrive from random directions with a range of speeds, whereas the hailstones lose kinetic energy and all arrive vertically.',
  ['C1: Δp = m(u + v) because the direction reverses', 'C1: F = rate × Δp', 'C1: p = F/A', 'B1: elastic and random for gas molecules'], aq='p')
N('H', 6, 'In a simple model of a gas, 2 molecules move at 100 m s⁻¹, 5 molecules at 300 m s⁻¹ and 3 molecules at 500 m s⁻¹. (a) Calculate the mean speed. (b) Calculate the mean square speed. (c) Calculate the root-mean-square speed. (d) Explain why the r.m.s. speed is greater than the mean speed. Give only the answer to (c).', sqrt((2 * 100 ** 2 + 5 * 300 ** 2 + 3 * 500 ** 2) / 10), 'm s⁻¹',
  f'(a) Mean speed = (2 × 100 + 5 × 300 + 3 × 500) ÷ 10 = 320 m s⁻¹. (b) ⟨c²⟩ = (2 × 100² + 5 × 300² + 3 × 500²) ÷ 10 = {sf((2 * 100 ** 2 + 5 * 300 ** 2 + 3 * 500 ** 2) / 10)} m² s⁻². (c) c_rms = √({sf((2 * 100 ** 2 + 5 * 300 ** 2 + 3 * 500 ** 2) / 10, 4)}) = {sf(sqrt((2 * 100 ** 2 + 5 * 300 ** 2 + 3 * 500 ** 2) / 10))} m s⁻¹. (d) Squaring the speeds gives extra weight to the fastest molecules, so the r.m.s. value is pulled above the mean.',
  ['C1: mean speed 320 m s⁻¹', 'C1: sum of squares weighted by numbers', 'C1: ÷ 10 molecules', 'C1: square root', 'B1: squaring weights fast molecules'], aq='c_rms')
N('H', 5, 'The r.m.s. speed of nitrogen molecules at 0 °C is 493 m s⁻¹. (a) Show that the density of nitrogen at 1.01 × 10⁵ Pa and 0 °C is about 1.25 kg m⁻³. (b) A nitrogen molecule has mass 4.65 × 10⁻²⁶ kg. Calculate the number of molecules per cubic metre. (c) The nitrogen is heated at constant volume until the r.m.s. speed is 600 m s⁻¹. Calculate the new pressure. Give only the answer to (b).', 3 * 1.01e5 / 493 ** 2 / 4.65e-26, 'm⁻³',
  f'(a) ρ = 3p/⟨c²⟩ = 3 × 1.01 × 10⁵ ÷ 493² = {sf(3 * 1.01e5 / 493 ** 2)} kg m⁻³. (b) n = ρ/m = {sf(3 * 1.01e5 / 493 ** 2, 4)} ÷ 4.65 × 10⁻²⁶ = {sf(3 * 1.01e5 / 493 ** 2 / 4.65e-26)} m⁻³. (c) At constant density p ∝ ⟨c²⟩, so p = 1.01 × 10⁵ × (600 ÷ 493)² = {sf(1.01e5 * (600 / 493) ** 2)} Pa.',
  ['C1: ρ = 3p/⟨c²⟩', 'B1: value ≈ 1.25 kg m⁻³ shown', 'C1: n = ρ/m', 'M1: p ∝ ⟨c²⟩ at constant volume'], aq='n')
N('H', 6, 'A vertical cylinder is closed at the top by a frictionless piston of mass 4.0 kg and area 2.0 × 10⁻³ m². Atmospheric pressure is 1.0 × 10⁵ Pa. The gas below the piston has density 1.6 kg m⁻³. (a) Calculate the pressure of the gas when the piston is at rest. (b) Calculate the r.m.s. speed of the gas molecules. (c) The gas is warmed and the piston rises slowly. Explain, in terms of molecular motion, why the piston rises while the gas pressure stays the same. Give only the answer to (b).' + take('g'), sqrt(3 * (1.0e5 + 4.0 * g / 2.0e-3) / 1.6), 'm s⁻¹',
  f'(a) p = 1.0 × 10⁵ + 4.0 × 9.81 ÷ 2.0 × 10⁻³ = {sf(1e5 + 4 * 9.81 / 2e-3)} Pa. (b) ⟨c²⟩ = 3p/ρ, so c_rms = √(3 × {sf(1e5 + 4 * 9.81 / 2e-3, 4)} ÷ 1.6) = {sf(sqrt(3 * (1e5 + 4 * 9.81 / 2e-3) / 1.6))} m s⁻¹. (c) Faster molecules hit the piston harder, so it moves up; the gas expands, so the molecules hit each square metre less often, and the pressure returns to the value set by the weight and the atmosphere.',
  ['C1: pressure from piston = mg/A', 'C1: add atmospheric pressure', 'C1: ⟨c²⟩ = 3p/ρ', 'C1: square root', 'B1: harder impacts but less frequent as volume rises'], aq='c_rms')
N('H', 5, 'Air at 1.0 × 10⁵ Pa pushes on an area of 1.0 cm² of a container wall. In a simple model, every molecule (mass 4.8 × 10⁻²⁶ kg) strikes the wall at right angles at 480 m s⁻¹ and rebounds elastically. (a) Calculate the force on the 1.0 cm² area. (b) Calculate the change in momentum of one molecule in a collision. (c) Hence estimate the number of molecular collisions per second on the area. (d) Suggest why this model gives only an estimate. Give only the answer to (c), in s⁻¹.', 1.0e5 * 1.0e-4 / (2 * 4.8e-26 * 480), 's⁻¹',
  f'(a) F = pA = 1.0 × 10⁵ × 1.0 × 10⁻⁴ = 10 N. (b) Δp = 2mu = 2 × 4.8 × 10⁻²⁶ × 480 = {sf(2 * 4.8e-26 * 480)} kg m s⁻¹. (c) Collision rate = F ÷ Δp = 10 ÷ {sf(2 * 4.8e-26 * 480, 4)} = {sf(10 / (2 * 4.8e-26 * 480))} s⁻¹. (d) Real molecules hit at all angles and with a wide range of speeds, so the true rate differs from this single-speed, head-on estimate.',
  ['C1: F = pA with 1.0 cm² = 1.0 × 10⁻⁴ m²', 'C1: Δp = 2mu', 'C1: rate = F ÷ Δp', 'B1: range of speeds and angles'], aq='rate')

# ── extra questions to reach 50 (20.3) ──
lesson('20.3')
N('M', 3, 'Copper has density 8960 kg m⁻³ and molar mass 0.0635 kg mol⁻¹. Calculate the volume occupied by one mole of copper, in cm³.', 0.0635 / 8960 * 1e6, 'cm³',
  f'V = M/ρ = 0.0635 ÷ 8960 = {sf(0.0635 / 8960)} m³. In cm³: {sf(0.0635 / 8960, 4)} × 10⁶ = {sf(0.0635 / 8960 * 1e6)}',
  ['C1: V = M/ρ', 'C1: m³ → cm³ (× 10⁶)'], aq='V')
N('H', 6, 'Sodium chloride has density 2160 kg m⁻³ and molar mass 0.0585 kg mol⁻¹. Each formula unit, NaCl, is made of one Na⁺ ion and one Cl⁻ ion. (a) Calculate the amount, in moles, of NaCl in 1.0 cm³ of the crystal. (b) Calculate the number of ions in 1.0 cm³. (c) Assuming each ion occupies a small cube, estimate the distance between neighbouring ions. Give only the answer to (c), in nm.' + take('NA'),
  (1e-6 / (2 * 2160e-6 / 0.0585 * NA_)) ** (1 / 3) * 1e9, 'nm',
  f'(a) m = 2160 × 1.0 × 10⁻⁶ = {sf(2160e-6)} kg. n = {sf(2160e-6)} ÷ 0.0585 = {sf(2160e-6 / 0.0585)} mol. (b) Two ions per formula unit: N = 2 × {sf(2160e-6 / 0.0585, 4)} × 6.02 × 10²³ = {sf(2 * 2160e-6 / 0.0585 * NA_)}. (c) Volume per ion = 1.0 × 10⁻⁶ ÷ {sf(2 * 2160e-6 / 0.0585 * NA_, 4)} = {sf(1e-6 / (2 * 2160e-6 / 0.0585 * NA_))} m³. Spacing = ({sf(1e-6 / (2 * 2160e-6 / 0.0585 * NA_), 4)})^(1/3) = {sf((1e-6 / (2 * 2160e-6 / 0.0585 * NA_)) ** (1 / 3))} m. In nm: {sf((1e-6 / (2 * 2160e-6 / 0.0585 * NA_)) ** (1 / 3), 4)} × 10⁹ = {sf((1e-6 / (2 * 2160e-6 / 0.0585 * NA_)) ** (1 / 3) * 1e9)}',
  ['C1: m = ρV', 'C1: n = m/M', 'M1: two ions per formula unit', 'C1: volume per ion', 'C1: cube root'], aq='d')
N('H', 6, 'A current of 2.0 A passes for 30 minutes through copper sulfate solution, depositing copper on the cathode. Each Cu²⁺ ion needs two electrons to become a copper atom. Copper has molar mass 0.0635 kg mol⁻¹. (a) Calculate the charge carried by one mole of electrons. (b) Calculate the number of copper atoms deposited. (c) Calculate the mass of copper deposited, in mg. Give only the answer to (c).' + take('NA', 'e'),
  2.0 * 1800 / 1.60e-19 / 2 / NA_ * 0.0635 * 1e6, 'mg',
  f'(a) Charge per mole = 6.02 × 10²³ × 1.60 × 10⁻¹⁹ = {sf(NA_ * 1.6e-19)} C. (b) Q = It = 2.0 × 1800 = 3600 C. Electrons = 3600 ÷ 1.60 × 10⁻¹⁹ = {sf(3600 / 1.6e-19)}. Atoms = {sf(3600 / 1.6e-19)} ÷ 2 = {sf(3600 / 1.6e-19 / 2)}. (c) m = {sf(3600 / 1.6e-19 / 2, 4)} ÷ 6.02 × 10²³ × 0.0635 = {sf(3600 / 1.6e-19 / 2 / NA_ * 0.0635)} kg. In mg: {sf(3600 / 1.6e-19 / 2 / NA_ * 0.0635, 4)} × 10⁶ = {sf(3600 / 1.6e-19 / 2 / NA_ * 0.0635 * 1e6)}',
  ['C1: charge per mole = N_A e', 'C1: Q = It with t in seconds', 'C1: number of electrons = Q/e', 'M1: two electrons per atom', 'C1: m = (N/N_A) × M'], aq='m')
N('H', 5, 'In an early estimate of the Avogadro constant, a radium source emitted 1.36 × 10¹¹ α-particles per second. Each α-particle collected two electrons and became a helium atom. In one year (3.15 × 10⁷ s), 1.58 × 10⁻⁷ m³ of helium was collected, measured at a temperature and pressure at which one mole of gas occupies 0.0224 m³. (a) Calculate the number of helium atoms collected. (b) Calculate the amount of helium in moles. (c) Hence estimate the Avogadro constant. (d) Suggest one reason why the estimate differs slightly from the accepted value. Give only the answer to (c).',
  1.36e11 * 3.15e7 / (1.58e-7 / 0.0224), 'mol⁻¹',
  f'(a) N = 1.36 × 10¹¹ × 3.15 × 10⁷ = {sf(1.36e11 * 3.15e7)}. (b) n = 1.58 × 10⁻⁷ ÷ 0.0224 = {sf(1.58e-7 / 0.0224)} mol. (c) N_A = N/n = {sf(1.36e11 * 3.15e7, 4)} ÷ {sf(1.58e-7 / 0.0224, 4)} = {sf(1.36e11 * 3.15e7 / (1.58e-7 / 0.0224))} mol⁻¹. (d) A very small volume of gas is hard to measure accurately, and some helium may leak away or stay trapped in the source.',
  ['C1: N = rate × time', 'C1: n = V/V_m', 'M1: N_A = N/n', 'B1: sensible source of error'], aq='N_A')
N('H', 6, 'Gold leaf of thickness 0.10 µm covers an area of 1.0 × 10⁻² m². Gold has density 19 300 kg m⁻³ and molar mass 0.197 kg mol⁻¹. (a) Calculate the mass of the leaf. (b) Calculate the number of gold atoms in the leaf. (c) Assuming each atom occupies a small cube, estimate the diameter of a gold atom. (d) Hence estimate how many layers of atoms make up the thickness of the leaf. Give only the answer to (d).' + take('NA'),
  1.0e-7 / (0.197 / (19300 * NA_)) ** (1 / 3), None,
  f'(a) m = 19 300 × 1.0 × 10⁻² × 1.0 × 10⁻⁷ = {sf(19300 * 1e-9)} kg. (b) N = {sf(19300 * 1e-9)} ÷ 0.197 × 6.02 × 10²³ = {sf(19300e-9 / 0.197 * NA_)}. (c) Volume per atom = 0.197 ÷ (19 300 × 6.02 × 10²³) = {sf(0.197 / (19300 * NA_))} m³. d = ({sf(0.197 / (19300 * NA_), 4)})^(1/3) = {sf((0.197 / (19300 * NA_)) ** (1 / 3))} m. (d) Layers = 1.0 × 10⁻⁷ ÷ {sf((0.197 / (19300 * NA_)) ** (1 / 3), 4)} = {sf(1e-7 / (0.197 / (19300 * NA_)) ** (1 / 3))}',
  ['C1: m = ρ × area × thickness', 'C1: N = (m/M)N_A', 'C1: volume per atom = M/(ρN_A)', 'C1: d = cube root', 'C1: layers = thickness ÷ d'], aq='layers')
N('H', 5, 'Natural chlorine is 75.8 % chlorine-35 (atomic mass 34.97 u) and 24.2 % chlorine-37 (atomic mass 36.97 u), by number of atoms. (a) Calculate the mean mass of a chlorine atom, in u. (b) Calculate the molar mass of chlorine atoms, using 1 u = 1.66 × 10⁻²⁷ kg. (c) Calculate the number of chlorine-37 atoms in 1.0 g of natural chlorine. Give only the answer to (c).' + take('NA'),
  0.242 * 1.0e-3 / ((0.758 * 34.97 + 0.242 * 36.97) * 1.66e-27), None,
  f'(a) Mean mass = 0.758 × 34.97 + 0.242 × 36.97 = {0.758 * 34.97 + 0.242 * 36.97:.2f} u. (b) M = {0.758 * 34.97 + 0.242 * 36.97:.2f} × 1.66 × 10⁻²⁷ × 6.02 × 10²³ = {sf((0.758 * 34.97 + 0.242 * 36.97) * 1.66e-27 * NA_)} kg mol⁻¹. (c) Total atoms = 1.0 × 10⁻³ ÷ ({0.758 * 34.97 + 0.242 * 36.97:.2f} × 1.66 × 10⁻²⁷) = {sf(1e-3 / ((0.758 * 34.97 + 0.242 * 36.97) * 1.66e-27))}. Atoms of the heavier isotope = 0.242 × {sf(1e-3 / ((0.758 * 34.97 + 0.242 * 36.97) * 1.66e-27), 4)} = {sf(0.242 * 1e-3 / ((0.758 * 34.97 + 0.242 * 36.97) * 1.66e-27))}',
  ['C1: weighted mean mass', 'C1: M = mean mass × u × N_A', 'C1: total atoms in 1.0 g', 'M1: × 0.242'], aq='N')

# ── extra questions to reach 50 (20.4) ──
lesson('20.4')
N('H', 5, 'A diving bell is a cylinder of height 3.0 m, closed at the top and open at the bottom. It is full of air at 1.0 × 10⁵ Pa when its lower edge touches the sea surface. It is lowered slowly until the sea water has risen 1.0 m inside it. The temperature of the trapped air stays constant. Sea water has density 1030 kg m⁻³ and atmospheric pressure is 1.0 × 10⁵ Pa. (a) Calculate the pressure of the trapped air. (b) Calculate the depth of the water surface inside the bell below the sea surface. Give only the answer to (b).' + take('g'),
  (1.0e5 * 3.0 / 2.0 - 1.0e5) / (1030 * g), 'm',
  f'(a) The bell has a uniform cross-section, so V ∝ length of the air column: p₂ = 1.0 × 10⁵ × 3.0 ÷ 2.0 = 1.5 × 10⁵ Pa. (b) p₂ = p₀ + ρgh, so h = (1.5 × 10⁵ − 1.0 × 10⁵) ÷ (1030 × 9.81) = {sf(0.5e5 / (1030 * 9.81))} m',
  ['C1: Boyle\'s law with V ∝ air-column length', 'C1: p₂ = 1.5 × 10⁵ Pa', 'C1: p = p₀ + ρgh', 'C1: rearranged for h'], aq='h')
N('H', 6, 'A car tyre has a fixed volume of 2000 cm³ and contains air at 1.0 × 10⁵ Pa. A pump delivers 150 cm³ of atmospheric air (1.0 × 10⁵ Pa) with each stroke. Assume the temperature stays constant. (a) Calculate the volume that the air in the tyre at a final pressure of 3.0 × 10⁵ Pa would occupy at 1.0 × 10⁵ Pa. (b) Hence calculate the volume of atmospheric air that must be pumped in. (c) Calculate the number of strokes needed. (d) In practice the air warms as it is pumped. Explain how the pressure changes after pumping stops and the tyre cools. Give only the answer to (c).',
  27, None,
  '(a) V = 3.0 × 10⁵ × 2000 ÷ 1.0 × 10⁵ = 6000 cm³. (b) Air to add = 6000 − 2000 = 4000 cm³. (c) Strokes = 4000 ÷ 150 = 26.7, so 27 strokes are needed. (d) As the trapped air cools at constant volume its molecules slow down and hit the walls less hard and less often, so the pressure falls below the value reached while it was warm.',
  ['C1: p₁V₁ = p₂V₂ for the final air', 'C1: 6000 cm³', 'C1: subtract the 2000 cm³ already present', 'C1: ÷ 150 cm³ per stroke', 'B1: pressure falls as the air cools'], sig=2, aq='number of strokes')
N('H', 5, 'A fixed mass of gas is compressed at constant temperature. A student records: p = 1.0 × 10⁵ Pa, V = 6.00 × 10⁻⁴ m³; p = 2.0 × 10⁵ Pa, V = 3.00 × 10⁻⁴ m³; p = 4.0 × 10⁵ Pa, V = 1.52 × 10⁻⁴ m³; p = 8.0 × 10⁵ Pa, V = 0.78 × 10⁻⁴ m³. (a) Calculate pV for each reading. (b) State and explain whether the gas obeys Boyle\'s law over the whole range. (c) Use the first reading to predict the volume at 8.0 × 10⁵ Pa for an ideal gas, and calculate the percentage by which the measured volume exceeds it. Give only the percentage from (c), in %.',
  (0.78e-4 - 60.0 / 8.0e5) / (60.0 / 8.0e5) * 100, '%',
  f'(a) First reading: 1.0 × 10⁵ × 6.00 × 10⁻⁴ = 60.0 J. Second reading: 2.0 × 10⁵ × 3.00 × 10⁻⁴ = 60.0 J. Third reading: 4.0 × 10⁵ × 1.52 × 10⁻⁴ = 60.8 J. Fourth reading: 8.0 × 10⁵ × 0.78 × 10⁻⁴ = 62.4 J. (b) pV is constant at low pressure but rises at the higher pressures, so the gas departs from Boyle\'s law when it is strongly compressed. (c) Ideal volume: 60.0 ÷ 8.0 × 10⁵ = 7.50 × 10⁻⁵ m³. Percentage excess: (0.78 × 10⁻⁴ − 7.50 × 10⁻⁵) ÷ 7.50 × 10⁻⁵ × 100 = {sf((0.78e-4 - 7.5e-5) / 7.5e-5 * 100, 2)} %',
  ['C1: pV values calculated', 'B1: pV not constant at high pressure', 'C1: ideal V = 60.0 ÷ 8.0 × 10⁵', 'C1: percentage difference'], sig=2, aq='percentage excess')
